window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["jammu-2020"] = {"id": "jammu-2020", "title": "University of Jammu 2020", "fullTitle": "University of Jammu 2020, M.A. Psychology entrance (real paper)", "sub": "60 real questions · no official key, every answer worked out from standard texts · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics", "aptitude": "General Awareness"}, "questions": [{"q": "Influential model of attitude change and formation is:", "options": {"a": "Value likelihood model", "b": "Expectancy elaboration model", "c": "Value elaboration model", "d": "Elaboration likelihood model"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: the elaboration likelihood model (ELM) is the influential model of attitude change and formation. Richard Petty and John Cacioppo (1986) set it out after a run of studies from the early 1980s. Elaboration means thinking carefully about the arguments in a message, and the likelihood of elaboration depends on two things: motivation (the issue matters to you, or you enjoy thinking) and ability (time, knowledge, no distraction). When both are high, people take the central route: they weigh the quality of the arguments, and the attitude that results is strong, lasting, resistant to counter-attack and good at predicting behaviour. When either is low, they take the peripheral route: they lean on cues such as an attractive or famous source, the sheer number of arguments or their own good mood, and the change is weaker and shorter-lived. In the classic study by Richard Petty, John Cacioppo and Rachel Goldman (1981), students who thought a new exam policy would affect them personally were moved by strong arguments and not by weak ones, while students for whom it was irrelevant were swayed mainly by the prestige of the source. Example: Arjun, buying his first laptop, compares battery tests and benchmark scores (central route); his cousin, who does not care much, buys the one a cricket star advertises (peripheral route). Option d is right because it is the exact name of this two-route model of persuasion. Exam trap: the three wrong options are made-up blends of real words, and 'expectancy' and 'value' borrow from expectancy-value models of attitude structure; the only genuine rival dual-process model is Shelly Chaiken's (1980) heuristic-systematic model, which is not offered here.", "topic": "Elaboration likelihood model", "fig": "Richard Petty and John Cacioppo"}, {"q": "All of the following are dimensions of Kelley's attribution theory except:", "options": {"a": "Consensus", "b": "Consistency", "c": "Distinctiveness", "d": "Constancy"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: constancy is not one of the dimensions in Harold Kelley's attribution theory. Harold Kelley (1967) proposed the covariation model: to decide whether a behaviour was caused by the person (an internal attribution) or by the stimulus or situation (an external attribution), we check how the behaviour varies across three kinds of information. Consensus asks whether other people behave the same way towards the same stimulus. Consistency asks whether this person behaves this way towards this stimulus on other occasions. Distinctiveness asks whether the person behaves this way only towards this stimulus or towards many others too. Example: Kabir laughs loudly at a stand-up comic. If everyone in the hall laughs (high consensus), Kabir laughs at this comic every time (high consistency) and he rarely laughs at other comics (high distinctiveness), we decide the comic is genuinely funny: an external attribution. If nobody else laughs (low consensus), Kabir always laughs at this comic (high consistency) and he laughs at every comic (low distinctiveness), we decide it is something about Kabir: an internal attribution. When consistency is low, we put the behaviour down to passing circumstances. For one-off observations Kelley later added causal schemas such as discounting. Option d is right because constancy is a term from perception (size, shape and colour constancy: seeing objects as stable although their image on the retina changes), not a source of attributional information. Exam trap: examiners use 'constancy' because it sounds like 'consistency', and they also reverse definitions; consistency is the same person over time, distinctiveness is the same person across different stimuli, and consensus is different people with the same stimulus.", "topic": "Kelley's covariation model", "fig": "Harold Kelley"}, {"q": "Dissonance can be described as:", "options": {"a": "Motivational state", "b": "State of arousal", "c": "Both (A) and (B)", "d": "Neither (A) nor (B)"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: cognitive dissonance is both a motivational state and a state of arousal. Leon Festinger (1957) defined dissonance as the unpleasant tension we feel when two of our cognitions (beliefs, attitudes or knowledge of our own behaviour) clash, such as 'smoking kills' and 'I smoke'. Like hunger, this tension works as a drive: it motivates us to reduce it by changing a belief or behaviour, adding consonant thoughts ('my grandfather smoked and lived to ninety') or playing down the importance of the clash. In the classic study by Leon Festinger and J. Merrill Carlsmith (1959), students paid only one dollar to tell the next participant that a dull task was fun later rated the task as more enjoyable than students paid twenty dollars, because they had too little outside justification for the lie. Dissonance is also physiological arousal. Mark Zanna and Joel Cooper (1974) gave participants a placebo pill; those told the pill would make them tense could blame their arousal on it, and the usual attitude change disappeared. Robert Croyle and Joel Cooper (1983) then recorded raised skin conductance in people who freely wrote essays against their own views. Example: Meera, who prides herself on saving money, buys an expensive phone; she feels uneasy and keeps telling herself that the camera will save her hiring a photographer. Option c is right because dissonance is a drive-like motivational state and an arousal state at the same time. Exam trap: Daryl Bem's (1967) self-perception theory explained the same attitude-change results with no arousal at all, which is why some students think dissonance is purely cognitive; the pill and skin conductance studies showed that the arousal is real and drives the change.", "topic": "Cognitive dissonance as arousal", "fig": "Leon Festinger"}, {"q": "According to Jones and Davis correspondent inference theory, we find person's action to be most informative about variant dispositions when:", "options": {"a": "The action depends on the person's situation", "b": "The action is judged to be intentional", "c": "The action seems to be caused by a consistent goal", "d": "Both (B) and (C)"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: by correspondent inference theory, an action tells us most about a person's stable dispositions when it is judged to be intentional. Edward Jones and Keith Davis (1965) asked when we can infer that an act 'corresponds' to an underlying trait, so that a hostile act reveals a hostile person. The first step is to decide that the act was intended: the actor knew what the consequences would be and had the ability to bring them about. Accidents tell us nothing about character. The inference then gets stronger when the act was freely chosen rather than forced by the situation, when it produces non-common effects (outcomes that only this choice brings, which pinpoint the motive), and when it is low in social desirability, because doing the expected thing (thanking a host) reveals little while out-of-role behaviour reveals a lot. Hedonic relevance (the act affects us) and personalism (it seems aimed at us) strengthen it further. Example: if Riya knocks her classmate's coffee over his notes on purpose, knowing what will happen, we infer hostility; if someone jogged her elbow, we learn nothing about her. Option b is right because judged intentionality is the gateway to a correspondent inference. Exam trap: 'consistent' belongs to Harold Kelley's (1967) covariation model (the same behaviour over time), not to Jones and Davis, so a 'both' option pairing intention with a consistent goal looks plausible but mixes two theories. Key note: the printed 'variant dispositions' is almost certainly a slip for 'invariant' (stable) dispositions, which is what the theory is about; since c is not a Jones and Davis condition, d falls with it, and the answer used here, b, stands.", "topic": "Correspondent inference theory", "fig": "Edward Jones and Keith Davis"}, {"q": "The reinforcement affect model predicts that:", "options": {"a": "The confederate is liked more in the gain condition", "b": "People will be attracted to someone whom they associate with a good feeling", "c": "Liking someone while disagreeing with that person is psychologically uncomfortable", "d": "The confederate is disliked more in the loss conditions"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: the reinforcement-affect model predicts that people will be attracted to someone they associate with a good feeling. Donn Byrne and Gerald Clore (1970) built this model on classical conditioning. Things that make us feel good (praise, a comfortable room, good news) act as rewards, and anyone who happens to be present gets linked with the pleasant feeling, so we like them, even if they did not cause it. Bad feelings work the other way. William Griffitt (1970) found that students rated an unseen stranger less favourably when they sat in an uncomfortably hot room than in a comfortable one, although the stranger had nothing to do with the heat. Example: Kabir is introduced to Anjali at the party where he hears that he has topped the exam; weeks later he still feels warmly towards her without knowing why. The same idea explains why a first meeting in a pleasant café tends to go better than one at a noisy, crowded bus stand. Option b is right because liking through association with positive feelings is the model's core prediction. Exam trap: the other three options are predictions of different theories from the same chapter on attraction. Options a and d come from the gain-loss study of Elliot Aronson and Darwyn Linder (1965), in which a confederate whose remarks about the participant moved from negative to positive was liked most, and one whose remarks moved from positive to negative was liked least. Option c is Fritz Heider's (1958) balance theory.", "topic": "Reinforcement-affect model of attraction", "fig": "Donn Byrne and Gerald Clore"}, {"q": "Volunteer process model was given by:", "options": {"a": "Batson and Gray", "b": "Omoto and Snyder", "c": "Schmidt and Tomasello", "d": "Richerson and Boyd"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: the volunteer process model was given by Allen Omoto and Mark Snyder. Allen Omoto and Mark Snyder (1995) studied people who volunteered to support those living with AIDS, often for months or years and with no obligation to do so. Their model treats volunteering as a process with three stages. Antecedents are what the person brings: personality (such as empathy and a helping disposition), motives, and life circumstances such as existing social support. Experiences are what happens during service: satisfaction with the work and integration into the organisation. Consequences are the outcomes: how long the person keeps serving, and changes in attitudes and knowledge. Using a functional approach, they identified five motives: values (humanitarian concern), understanding (learning about the illness), personal development, community concern and esteem enhancement. Interestingly, the more self-oriented motives (understanding, personal development, esteem) predicted who stayed longer better than purely other-oriented values. Example: Riya joins an NGO that supports cancer patients because she wants to learn about the illness and feel useful; if she is satisfied and feels part of the team, the model predicts she will still be volunteering a year later. Option b is right because Omoto and Snyder are the authors of this three-stage model of sustained volunteering. Exam trap: the distractors pair real researchers who work on other prosocial or cooperation topics, so the names look familiar from the same chapter, but none of them proposed a model of volunteering.", "topic": "Volunteer process model", "fig": "Allen Omoto and Mark Snyder"}, {"q": "_____ is the form of prosocial behaviour, in which individual tries to reduce guilt and compensate for the harm although the effect isn't always direct at the person who was harmed.", "options": {"a": "The warm glow of success", "b": "Reparative altruism", "c": "Image-repair hypothesis", "d": "Altruism hypothesis"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: reparative altruism is prosocial behaviour aimed at reducing guilt and making up for harm, even when the help does not reach the person who was harmed. After we hurt someone, guilt is unpleasant, and helping repairs our sense of being a decent person. The striking point is that the repair need not be directed at the victim. J. Merrill Carlsmith and Alan Gross (1969) led participants to believe they had given painful electric shocks to a fellow participant (in fact a confederate) in a learning task. Later, when asked to make telephone calls for a campaign to save redwood trees, these 'guilty' participants agreed far more often than those who had only sounded a harmless buzzer, and compliance was highest when the request came not from the supposed victim but from someone who had watched them give the shocks. Example: Arjun snaps rudely at his younger brother before leaving home; on the way to college he gives the auto driver an unusually large tip and helps a stranger carry her bags. Option b is right because the stem lists the defining features: guilt reduction, compensation for harm, and help that is not always aimed at the person harmed. Exam trap: the image-repair idea also links wrongdoing to later helping, so examiners place it beside reparative altruism; but image repair is about restoring how others see you, while reparative altruism is about easing your own guilt. Key note: guilt-driven helping is sometimes discussed under the image-repair hypothesis, but the stem's wording (reduce guilt, compensate, not always aimed at the victim) is the definition of reparative altruism, so the answer used here, b, stands.", "topic": "Reparative altruism and guilt", "fig": ""}, {"q": "\"Aggressive energy\" held in check by inhibiting forces must be eventually discharged. Freud referred to such discharge as:", "options": {"a": "Displacement", "b": "Frustration", "c": "Reactive aggression", "d": "Catharsis"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: Sigmund Freud called the discharge of pent-up aggressive energy catharsis. Sigmund Freud (1920), after the slaughter of the First World War, added a death instinct (thanatos) to his theory and held what is often called a hydraulic view of aggression: aggressive energy builds up inside, like water behind a dam, and if inhibiting forces hold it in check it must eventually be released, either in harmful acts or in safer outlets such as sport, shouting or fantasy. That release is catharsis, a Greek word for cleansing that Aristotle used for the emotional purging an audience feels at a tragedy, and that Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud (1895) used for the 'cathartic method' of talking out buried feelings. Konrad Lorenz (1966) later proposed a similar hydraulic model. Research does not support venting. Brad Bushman (2002) angered students with harsh comments on their essays, then had some hit a punching bag while thinking about the critic; afterwards they were more aggressive, not less, than students who had sat quietly. Example: Kabir is told to 'let it out' by punching pillows after a quarrel with a friend, and he ends up angrier. Option d is right because catharsis is Freud's term for discharging held-back aggressive energy. Exam trap: displacement also gives aggression an outlet, so it is the near miss; but displacement is about the target changing (the boss's anger lands on the dog), while catharsis is about the stored energy being released and supposedly used up.", "topic": "Catharsis and aggressive energy", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "Constructive conflict resolution occurs:", "options": {"a": "When reciprocal communication is designed to reach agreement and situation in which some interests are shared and some are in opposition", "b": "When mutual compromise and concessions are made to carve up a fixed size pie", "c": "When two parties cooperate and find a mutually acceptable alternative to their incompatible goals", "d": "When one party gains and other loses"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: constructive conflict resolution occurs when two parties cooperate and find a mutually acceptable alternative to their incompatible goals. Morton Deutsch (1973), in The Resolution of Conflict, separated two kinds of conflict process. In destructive conflict the parties compete, communication breaks down, suspicion grows and the dispute escalates until one side wins and the other loses, or both lose. In constructive conflict the parties treat the dispute as a shared problem, communicate openly and look for a solution that satisfies both, so the relationship survives and often improves. The best result is an integrative agreement (Dean Pruitt, 1981), which 'expands the pie' by trading on issues that each side values differently instead of simply splitting the difference. Example: Meera wants the flat quiet for studying in the evenings and her flatmate Priya wants to practise the guitar; instead of fighting, they agree that Priya will practise in the mornings while Meera is at the library, so both get what they really need. Option c is right because cooperation that produces a mutually acceptable alternative is the definition of a constructive outcome. Exam trap: option a sounds positive because it mentions agreement, but it describes bargaining in a mixed-motive situation, the process of negotiating, not the constructive result; option b, a compromise over a fixed pie, still leaves each side with only part of what it wanted.", "topic": "Constructive conflict resolution", "fig": "Morton Deutsch"}, {"q": "Fiedler proposed _____ of leadership based on ideas that there are two basic styles of leadership.", "options": {"a": "Expert power", "b": "Contingent theory", "c": "Social learning theory", "d": "Cognitive neo-associationist model"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: Fred Fiedler proposed the contingency theory (contingency model) of leadership, built on the idea that there are two basic leadership styles. Fred Fiedler (1967) argued that no single style is best; effectiveness is contingent on the fit between the leader's style and the situation. Style is measured with the Least Preferred Co-worker (LPC) scale, on which the leader rates the person they have worked with least well. A leader who still describes that person fairly kindly (high LPC) is relationship-oriented; one who rates them harshly (low LPC) is task-oriented. Situational control, or favourableness, depends on three factors: leader-member relations (the most important), task structure, and position power (formal authority to reward and punish). Task-oriented leaders do best when control is very high or very low; relationship-oriented leaders do best when it is moderate. Because Fiedler saw style as a fairly fixed trait, he advised changing the situation to suit the leader rather than retraining the leader. Example: Arjun, a task-oriented captain, does well both with a chaotic new team that needs firm direction and with a loyal team on a clear-cut task, but his relationship-oriented friend Kavya does better with a fairly cooperative team facing a vague problem. Option b is right because 'contingent theory' is the stem's version of Fiedler's contingency model. Exam trap: the other options are real terms from the same syllabus, taken from power, learning and aggression, so they feel familiar, but none is a leadership theory built on two styles.", "topic": "Fiedler's contingency model", "fig": "Fred Fiedler"}, {"q": "Whether or not disadvantaged, people try to change what is perceived as unfair social structure depends on:", "options": {"a": "Stability and sociability of the social structure", "b": "Morality and permeability of the social structure", "c": "Permeability and stability of the social structure", "d": "Competence and morality of the social structure"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: whether members of a disadvantaged group try to change an unfair social structure depends on the permeability and stability of that structure. Henri Tajfel and John Turner (1979), in social identity theory, argued that people seek a positive social identity from the groups they belong to. When their group compares badly with others, two features of the status system decide how they respond. Permeability is whether group boundaries can be crossed: if an individual can move up into the higher-status group, people choose social mobility, leaving the group as individuals instead of challenging the system. Stability is whether the status order looks fixed or open to change, and it works together with legitimacy (whether the order looks fair): if boundaries are closed and the hierarchy looks unstable and illegitimate, so that a different order can be imagined, people turn to social competition, collectively challenging the dominant group. If the hierarchy looks stable and legitimate, they fall back on social creativity, comparing on a new dimension ('we are poorer but kinder'). Example: Kabir studies at a low-ranked college. If good marks let him transfer to a top college (permeable boundaries), he works for his own transfer; if transfers are closed but the ranking system looks unfair and open to challenge (unstable), his classmates organise together to demand recognition. Option c is right because permeability and stability are exactly the two structural variables the theory names. Exam trap: the distractors borrow dimensions from stereotype research, so they sound scholarly; warmth (sociability) and competence are the two dimensions of the stereotype content model, and morality is a dimension on which groups are judged, not a property of the social structure.", "topic": "Social identity: permeability and stability", "fig": "Henri Tajfel and John Turner"}, {"q": "One way to reverse prejudice as shown in the Robbers Cave experiment is to:", "options": {"a": "Foster shared goals", "b": "Educate the groups", "c": "Encourage competition", "d": "Maintain intergroup contact"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: the Robbers Cave experiment showed that prejudice between groups can be reversed by fostering shared, or superordinate, goals. Muzafer Sherif (1954) and colleagues took 22 unacquainted boys aged about 11 to 12 to a summer camp at Robbers Cave State Park in Oklahoma. In phase 1, two groups lived apart, bonded and named themselves the Eagles and the Rattlers. In phase 2, a tournament with prizes (tug of war, baseball) produced name-calling, a burned flag and cabin raids: competition for scarce rewards created hostility, as realistic conflict theory predicts. In phase 3, the researchers first tried simple contact, such as shared meals and films, and it failed; meals turned into food fights. Hostility fell only when the staff arranged superordinate goals, which both groups wanted but neither could reach alone: restoring the camp's water supply, pooling money to rent a film and pulling a stalled food truck together. Friendships across the two groups rose afterwards. The full report appeared in 1961. Example: two rival hostel wings at Riya's college stop sniping at each other once they must jointly run the annual fest. Option a is right because shared goals that require cooperation are what reversed the prejudice. Exam trap: option d is tempting because contact can reduce prejudice, but at Robbers Cave mere contact during conflict made things worse; Gordon Allport's (1954) contact hypothesis says contact works only with equal status, common goals, cooperation and support from authorities.", "topic": "Robbers Cave and superordinate goals", "fig": "Muzafer Sherif"}, {"q": "Comorbidity explains the notion that:", "options": {"a": "People having same problem die at the same time", "b": "Sufferers exhibit a chronic fear of death", "c": "Depression always occur in winter", "d": "Differentially defined disorders can co-occur"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: comorbidity is the notion that differently defined disorders can occur together in the same person. In psychiatry, comorbidity means the presence of two or more distinct disorders at the same time, such as major depressive disorder with generalised anxiety disorder, or alcohol use disorder with post-traumatic stress disorder. In mental health it is the rule rather than the exception. The National Comorbidity Survey of Ronald Kessler and colleagues (1994) in the United States found that more than half of all lifetime disorders occurred in the roughly one person in seven who had a history of three or more disorders. Comorbidity matters for three reasons. It complicates diagnosis, because symptoms overlap. It worsens prognosis, since comorbid cases are usually more severe and harder to treat. And it raises questions about classification: if anxiety and depression so often travel together, perhaps they share causes, which is one reason for transdiagnostic treatments. Example: Meera is diagnosed with panic disorder and, a year later, also meets the criteria for major depression; her clinician has to plan treatment for both, not just one. Option d is right because the co-occurrence of separately defined disorders in one individual is exactly what comorbidity means. Exam trap: 'morbidity' sounds like 'mortality', so examiners offer options about dying or death; comorbidity is about illnesses co-existing in a living person, and it is also different from prevalence, which is how common a disorder is in a population.", "topic": "Comorbidity", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the below given was historical explanation of psychopathology?", "options": {"a": "Witchcraft", "b": "General paresis", "c": "Plague", "d": "Social class"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: witchcraft was a historical explanation of psychopathology. David Barlow and Mark Durand, in their textbook Abnormal Psychology: An Integrative Approach, describe three traditions that have competed to explain abnormal behaviour. The supernatural tradition blamed outside agents: demons, evil spirits, witches and even the moon and stars (the root of the word 'lunatic', from the Latin luna, moon). In late medieval Europe, strange behaviour was often put down to possession or witchcraft, and the witch-hunters' manual Malleus Maleficarum (1486) by Heinrich Kramer guided trials; treatments included exorcism, prayer and sometimes torture or execution. The biological tradition began with the Greek physician Hippocrates (born about 460 BC), who traced disorders to the brain and to an imbalance of the four humours. The psychological tradition includes moral therapy, psychoanalysis and behaviourism. Example: in a story told by Riya's grandmother, a young bride's sudden fits were blamed on a neighbour's black magic and an exorcist was called instead of a doctor; that is the supernatural explanation at work. Option a is right because witchcraft is a cause that people actually put forward to explain disordered behaviour. Exam trap: general paresis is the famous item from the history of psychiatry, so it feels like a historical explanation, but it is a disorder (syphilis of the brain), not an explanation; finding its cause gave strong support to the biological tradition, as when Richard von Krafft-Ebing (1897) reported that paretic patients inoculated with syphilitic material did not catch syphilis, so they must already have been infected. Key note: general paresis is part of the history of explanation, as the discovery that revived the biological tradition in the nineteenth century, but it is a disorder rather than an explanation, so the answer used here, a, stands.", "topic": "Supernatural tradition in psychopathology", "fig": ""}, {"q": "APA first published a predecessor of DSM as a statistical classification of institutionalized mental patients in:", "options": {"a": "1852", "b": "1905", "c": "1844", "d": "1952"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: 1844 is the year usually given for the first predecessor of the DSM, a statistical classification of institutionalised mental patients credited to the forerunner of the American Psychiatric Association. The APA began that year as the Association of Medical Superintendents of American Institutions for the Insane, a body of asylum heads; it became the American Medico-Psychological Association in 1892 and took its present name in 1921. Many summaries of DSM history credit this new body with a statistical classification of the patients in its asylums in that founding year, which is why 1844 is given as the date of the first predecessor of the DSM. The push for classification in the United States was statistical. The 1840 census used a single category, 'idiocy/insanity', and the 1880 census separated seven: mania, melancholia, monomania, paresis, dementia, dipsomania and epilepsy. In 1918 the association, with the National Committee for Mental Hygiene, issued the Statistical Manual for the Use of Institutions for the Insane, the direct ancestor of the DSM. The first Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, DSM-I, followed in 1952, shaped partly by the army's classification from the Second World War. DSM-II came in 1968 and DSM-III, with explicit criteria, in 1980. Option c is right because 1844 is the date given for that first statistical classification, the earliest predecessor of the DSM. Exam trap: 1952 is the most familiar date in DSM history, so students pick it, but 1952 is DSM-I itself, not its predecessor; 1905 is the year of the Binet-Simon intelligence scale, and 1852 is a filler. Key note: 1844 is firmly the founding year of the association, but the APA's own history of the DSM begins with the 1840 and 1880 censuses and does not describe an 1844 classification; the claim comes from widely repeated summaries. Of the options, 1844 is the only date tied to the APA's origins, so the answer used here, c, stands.", "topic": "History of the DSM", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Psychopathology is unlikely to result from the impact of any single factor. This is one of the features of:", "options": {"a": "Reciprocal gene-environment", "b": "Diathesis-stress", "c": "Mind and body interaction", "d": "Both (A) and (C)"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: the idea that psychopathology is unlikely to result from any single factor is a feature of the diathesis-stress model. A diathesis is a vulnerability, usually inherited but sometimes acquired (early trauma, a negative thinking style), that makes a person more likely to develop a disorder. The disorder appears only when a stressor, such as a loss, an illness or a major life change, acts on that vulnerability. Neither alone is usually enough: many people with the vulnerability never become ill because they never meet enough stress, and many people under severe stress stay well because they carry little vulnerability. The greater the vulnerability, the less stress it takes. Avshalom Caspi and colleagues (2003) gave a well-known example: people with a short version of the serotonin transporter gene were more likely to become depressed, but mainly if they had faced several stressful life events or childhood maltreatment. Example: Meera and her cousin both lose their jobs in the same month; Meera, whose mother and grandmother had major depression, becomes depressed, while her cousin, with no such vulnerability, recovers within weeks. Option b is right because the model rests on the claim that vulnerability and stress must combine. Exam trap: the reciprocal gene-environment model and mind-body interaction also belong to the modern integrative view, so a 'both' option looks safe; but they describe how genes shape environments and how mind and body affect each other, not the classic vulnerability-plus-trigger formula. Key note: the reciprocal gene-environment model also rejects a single cause, but the classic model built on the idea that no single factor is enough is the diathesis-stress model, so the answer used here, b, stands.", "topic": "Diathesis-stress model", "fig": ""}, {"q": "An adolescent dealing with unacceptable social inadequacy attempts to mask those feeling by seeking oral gratification. This is an example of:", "options": {"a": "Repression", "b": "Reaction formation", "c": "Rationalization", "d": "Regression"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: seeking oral gratification to cope with painful feelings of social inadequacy is an example of regression. Sigmund Freud (1894) first described defences as unconscious strategies the ego uses to protect itself from anxiety, and his daughter Anna Freud (1936) catalogued them in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence. Regression is retreating, under stress, to behaviour typical of an earlier, safer stage of development. Freud's first psychosexual stage is the oral stage (roughly the first year to eighteen months), in which pleasure centres on the mouth through sucking and feeding. So oral habits such as overeating, chewing pens, nail-biting, smoking or thumb-sucking are the classic signs of regression in an older person. The adolescent in the stem cannot face the feeling of being socially inadequate, so instead of dealing with it at an adult level he falls back on the comfort the mouth gave him as an infant. Other everyday examples are a six-year-old who starts wetting the bed again after a sibling is born, or an adult who sulks and throws a tantrum when criticised. Option d is right because returning to oral comforts under stress is the textbook pattern of regression. Exam trap: the word 'mask' pulls students towards reaction formation, which does hide a feeling; but reaction formation hides it by acting out the opposite (loud bravado covering inadequacy), not by returning to an earlier stage's comforts. Fixation is the related idea that part of the libido stays stuck at one stage.", "topic": "Regression as a defence", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Neurotransmitters that are studied in psychopathology include _____ which can produce states of high arousal and _____ which inhibit nerve impulse.", "options": {"a": "Norepinephrine, GABA", "b": "GABA, Dopamine", "c": "Epinephrine, Dopamine", "d": "Serotonin, Norepinephrine"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: norepinephrine can produce states of high arousal, and GABA inhibits nerve impulses. Norepinephrine (noradrenaline) is a catecholamine made mainly by neurons of the locus coeruleus, a small nucleus in the pons whose axons reach almost the whole brain. It raises alertness, vigilance and arousal, and in the body it is the sympathetic nervous system's transmitter for the fight-or-flight response. Overactivity of this system is linked to panic attacks and to the hyperarousal of anxiety and post-traumatic stress disorder. GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid) is the main inhibitory neurotransmitter of the brain. When it binds to GABA-A receptors, chloride ions flow into the neuron and make it less likely to fire. Low GABA activity is linked to anxiety, and benzodiazepines (such as diazepam) and alcohol calm people because they strengthen GABA's effect. Example: when Kabir hears a loud crash at night, his locus coeruleus fires and noradrenaline makes him instantly alert, heart pounding; a sedative such as diazepam would damp this down by boosting GABA. Option a is right because it puts the arousing transmitter in the first blank and the inhibitory one in the second, exactly as the stem requires. Exam trap: the options reuse the same few transmitters in different orders, testing whether you know which one inhibits; GABA (and glycine in the spinal cord) inhibits, glutamate is the main excitatory transmitter, and none of norepinephrine, epinephrine, dopamine or serotonin is the brain's classic inhibitor.", "topic": "Norepinephrine and GABA", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The relational self is a concept from social psychology that incorporates ideas from:", "options": {"a": "Interpersonal theories", "b": "Object relation and attachment theories", "c": "Exchange relationships", "d": "Communal relationships"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: the relational self incorporates ideas from object relations and attachment theories. Susan Andersen and Serena Chen (2002), in Psychological Review, proposed that the self is not one fixed structure but is partly made of 'self-with-other' knowledge. For each significant other (a parent, a sibling, a partner) we store a mental representation of that person, linked to a version of ourselves as we are with them. When we meet someone new who resembles a significant other, that representation is activated automatically, and we start to feel, expect and even act like the self we are with that person. Andersen calls this social-cognitive transference, a laboratory version of Freud's transference. The concept draws on two older traditions. Object relations theory (Melanie Klein, Ronald Fairbairn, Donald Winnicott) holds that we internalise representations of self and others from early relationships. John Bowlby's (1969) attachment theory holds that early care builds internal working models of the self and of others that guide later relationships. Example: Arjun's new manager has the same stern, quiet manner as his father; without realising it, Arjun becomes the anxious, eager-to-please son he was at home. Option b is right because these two theories supply the internalised self-other representations at the heart of the concept. Exam trap: exchange and communal relationships sound relational, but they are relationship types from Margaret Clark and Judson Mills (1979), not source theories of the relational self. Key note: Susan Andersen has written that her approach draws directly on Harry Stack Sullivan's (1953) interpersonal theory, so a has real truth, but the standard description names object relations and attachment theories, so the answer used here, b, stands.", "topic": "The relational self", "fig": "Susan Andersen and Serena Chen"}, {"q": "In the Stroop tasks, interference is measured by:", "options": {"a": "How long it takes to name the colour of ink in list of words", "b": "How long it takes to name the list of words", "c": "How long it takes to check the distortion in people", "d": "How long it takes people to change the feeling"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: in the Stroop task, interference is measured by how long it takes to name the colour of the ink in a list of words. John Ridley Stroop (1935) printed colour words in conflicting ink colours, so that the word RED appeared in green ink. When people simply read the words aloud, the clashing colours barely slowed them. But when they had to name the ink colour and ignore the word, they took about 74 percent longer than when naming the colours of plain patches. That extra time is the Stroop interference effect. The explanation is automaticity: for a literate adult, reading is so practised that a word's meaning comes to mind involuntarily and competes with the weaker, less practised colour-naming response, which then has to win against it. Naming the ink is therefore the slow, effortful task, and the time difference between conflicting words and neutral colour patches is the measure. The task is now widely used to test selective attention and executive control, and an 'emotional Stroop' version shows that anxious people are slower to name the ink of threat-related words. Example: Riya sees BLUE printed in yellow ink and must say 'yellow'; she hesitates because 'blue' jumps into her mind first. Option a is right because the slowing of colour naming is exactly what interference refers to. Exam trap: option b, reading the word list, is the task that shows almost no interference, because word reading is automatic; examiners swap the two tasks.", "topic": "Stroop interference", "fig": "John Ridley Stroop"}, {"q": "Which among the given options is a predominant evolutionary theory of phobias?", "options": {"a": "Non-associative fear acquisition", "b": "Learned fear response", "c": "Biological preparedness", "d": "Specific phobia acquisition"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: biological preparedness is the leading evolutionary theory of phobias. Martin Seligman (1971) argued that evolution has prepared humans to learn fears of things that threatened our ancestors, such as snakes, spiders, heights, deep water and angry faces, far more easily than fears of modern dangers such as electric sockets or cars. Prepared fears can form after a single bad experience, or even from watching someone else, and they resist extinction. This explains why phobias cluster around a short list of ancestral objects. Strong evidence comes from rhesus monkeys: Michael Cook and Susan Mineka (1989) showed laboratory-reared monkeys videos of other monkeys reacting with fear; they learned to fear toy snakes and toy crocodiles, but not flowers or toy rabbits edited into the same footage. Arne Ohman and Susan Mineka (2001) later described an evolved \"fear module\" in the brain that such cues trigger selectively. Example: Riya has never been bitten, yet one scream from her cousin at a garden snake leaves her with a lasting snake phobia, while a far more dangerous shock from a faulty switch leaves no phobia at all. Option c is right because preparedness is the evolutionary explanation that dominates textbook accounts of phobias. Exam trap: a learned fear response (option b) sounds scientific but is plain conditioning with no evolutionary claim, while the non-associative account (option a) is also evolutionary, so students who spot only that word may pick it. Key note: Ross Menzies and John Clarke (1995) proposed non-associative fear acquisition (reviewed by Richie Poulton and Ross Menzies, 2002), in which some fears (of heights, water, strangers) are innate and appear without any learning; it is evolutionary too, but a minority view, and preparedness remains the predominant evolutionary account, so c stands.", "topic": "Preparedness theory of phobias", "fig": "Martin Seligman"}, {"q": "Individuals with somatoform disorders may often display a surprising indifference about the symptoms especially when the symptoms to most people would be disturbing. This is known as:", "options": {"a": "viva la difference", "b": "quella difference", "c": "la belle indifference", "d": "quesce quasa la difference"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: la belle indifférence, French for \"beautiful indifference\", is the calm, almost cheerful unconcern some patients show about a dramatic physical symptom. It is classically described in conversion disorder, which DSM-IV placed among the somatoform disorders and DSM-5 calls functional neurological symptom disorder, now in the group of somatic symptom and related disorders. In conversion disorder a person loses a motor or sensory function (a paralysed arm, sudden blindness, loss of speech, seizures) with no neurological disease that explains it. The phrase comes from nineteenth-century work on hysteria: Sigmund Freud (1895) used it in Studies on Hysteria for his patient Elisabeth von R. and later credited it to his Paris teacher, the neurologist Jean-Martin Charcot. It is often wrongly credited to Pierre Janet, who wrote about such indifference but does not seem to have used the phrase. Example: Meera wakes up unable to move her right arm the morning after a bitter family quarrel. Doctors find normal reflexes and nerve tests, yet she smiles and says it is not really bothering her. Most people would be terrified; her calm is la belle indifférence. Option c is right because it is the standard clinical term for this surprising lack of concern. Exam trap: the sign is less useful than its fame suggests. DSM-5 calls it non-specific, and a systematic review by Jon Stone and colleagues (2006) found it in a median of 21 percent of conversion patients but 29 percent of patients with real organic disease, so it must never be used to rule out a neurological illness. The other three options are distortions of French or Italian phrases built to look like the real term.", "topic": "La belle indifférence", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In schizophrenia when an individual has disorganised speech, the term 'clanging' refers to:", "options": {"a": "Individuals only communicate with words that rhyme", "b": "Answers to questions may not be relevant", "c": "Individuals communicate without completing their sentences", "d": "Speech may be neither structured nor comprehensible"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: clanging means choosing words for their sound, especially rhyme, rather than their meaning. Disorganised speech is one of the five DSM-5 criterion A symptoms of schizophrenia, and it reflects formal thought disorder, a breakdown in the links between ideas that we infer from how a person talks. In clanging (clang associations) the next word is pulled in by how the last one sounded, so speech follows rhymes and puns instead of a line of thought. Example: asked how he slept, Arjun says, \"I slept, wept, kept the cat, the hat, the bat is flat.\" Each word echoes the sound of the one before, but the sentence goes nowhere. Clanging is also common in mania, where it appears inside flight of ideas, so it is not unique to schizophrenia. Option a is right because it is the only option built on sound, the defining feature of clanging; the word \"only\" overstates it, since clanging speech still mixes in ordinary words, but rhyme is the marker. Exam trap: all four options describe real kinds of disorganised speech, so the item tests whether you can match each label to its description. Irrelevant answers are tangentiality, unfinished sentences are thought blocking or derailment, and speech that is neither structured nor understandable is incoherence (word salad). Only clanging is defined by rhyme.", "topic": "Clanging in disorganised speech", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Cognitive therapy, when added to exposure for PTSD, is particularly helpful in addressing:", "options": {"a": "Suicidal tendencies", "b": "Risk of relapse", "c": "Depersonalization", "d": "Guilt"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: when cognitive therapy is added to exposure for post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD), its special contribution is reducing trauma-related guilt (and the shame that often goes with it). Exposure therapies, such as the prolonged exposure of Edna Foa and Barbara Rothbaum (1998), have the person repeatedly relive the trauma memory and face safe reminders, so fear habituates and avoidance drops. But fear is not the only emotion in PTSD. Many survivors blame themselves (\"I should have stopped it\", \"I froze, so it is my fault\"), and these appraisals do not always fade with exposure alone, because guilt rests on beliefs about responsibility rather than on a learned fear. Cognitive therapy targets those beliefs directly: the therapist helps the person examine hindsight bias (judging past choices with knowledge that came only later) and weigh what was really in their control. Cognitive processing therapy (Patricia Resick and Monica Schnicke, 1992) works on such \"stuck points\", and a 2002 trial by Resick's group with rape survivors found it matched prolonged exposure on PTSD symptoms and did better on two of four guilt subscales. Example: Kabir survived a road accident in which his friend died. After exposure sessions he can drive past the spot without panic, but he still thinks, \"I chose that road, so I killed him.\" Cognitive work on this belief eases his guilt. Option d is right because guilt is the problem on which cognitive techniques add most to exposure. Exam trap: suicidal risk and relapse sound like weighty clinical targets, so they tempt, but they are general concerns in every treatment, not the specific gain from adding cognitive therapy.", "topic": "Cognitive therapy for PTSD guilt", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following psychotherapies have obtained support in the treatment of MDD?", "options": {"a": "Interpersonal psychotherapy", "b": "Behavioural activation", "c": "Psychoanalytic therapy", "d": "Cognitive therapy"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: cognitive therapy has the largest body of research support among the listed psychotherapies for major depressive disorder (MDD). Aaron Beck, John Rush, Brian Shaw and Gary Emery (1979) built it on the idea that depression is kept going by negative automatic thoughts about the self, the world and the future (the cognitive triad). Therapist and client treat these thoughts as hypotheses, test them against evidence and through behavioural experiments, and replace distortions such as overgeneralisation. Many trials show it works about as well as antidepressants for most patients and protects better against relapse after treatment ends. Two other options are also evidence-based. Interpersonal psychotherapy, from Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman (1984), works on grief, role disputes, role transitions and interpersonal deficits; in the NIMH Treatment of Depression Collaborative Research Program (Irene Elkin and colleagues, 1989) it did about as well as cognitive behaviour therapy. Behavioural activation schedules rewarding activity: Neil Jacobson and colleagues (1996) found it as effective as full cognitive therapy, and Sona Dimidjian and colleagues (2006) found it matched medication in severe depression. Example: Arjun stops seeing friends after losing his job and thinks, \"I am useless.\" Cognitive therapy tests that thought; behavioural activation would get him out walking with friends again. Option d is right because cognitive therapy is the most researched psychological treatment for MDD. Exam trap: with three supported options and no \"all of these\", students freeze; pick the treatment with the largest evidence base, and never psychoanalytic therapy (option c), the only one with weak support. Key note: interpersonal psychotherapy and behavioural activation are also well supported, so three options are true and the item has no clean single answer; cognitive therapy is keyed as the most researched; d stands, and only c is plainly wrong.", "topic": "Evidence-based therapies for depression", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The most commonly used psychological treatment for obsessive-compulsive and related disorders is:", "options": {"a": "Coping strategies that prevent processing the problems", "b": "Imaginal exposure", "c": "In-vivo exposure", "d": "Exposure and response prevention"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: exposure and response prevention (ERP) is the most widely used and best supported psychological treatment for obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD), and it is also used for related disorders. Victor Meyer (1966) first described it for obsessional rituals, and Edna Foa and colleagues refined and tested it from the 1980s onwards. It has two parts. In exposure, the person deliberately faces the thought, object or situation that triggers the obsession, usually step by step up a hierarchy. In response prevention, the person does not perform the compulsion that normally follows. The logic is behavioural: the ritual brings quick relief, so it is negatively reinforced, and it stops the person from learning that the anxiety would fade on its own and the feared disaster would not happen. Blocking it allows habituation and new learning. Studies by Foa's team that tested the two parts separately found that exposure alone mainly lowered anxiety, response prevention alone mainly cut rituals, and the combination worked best. Example: Meera fears germs and washes her hands 40 times a day. Her therapist has her touch a dustbin lid and then sit without washing; her anxiety peaks, then falls over the hour, and peaks lower in the next session. Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors and clomipramine are the main drug options, but ERP is the first-line psychological treatment. Option d is right because it names the full package that research supports. Exam trap: imaginal exposure and in-vivo exposure are real parts of ERP, so they look correct, but exposure without response prevention leaves the ritual free to neutralise the fear, and gains are much weaker.", "topic": "Exposure and response prevention for OCD", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is helpful in GAD?", "options": {"a": "Relaxation and cognitive behavioural approach", "b": "Pharmacotherapy", "c": "Electroconvulsive treatment", "d": "Psychoanalytic therapy"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: a psychological treatment combining relaxation training with cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT) is the established helpful treatment for generalised anxiety disorder (GAD). In DSM-5, GAD is excessive, hard-to-control worry about many everyday matters on more days than not for at least six months, with symptoms such as restlessness, fatigue, poor concentration, irritability, muscle tension and disturbed sleep. Thomas Borkovec's avoidance theory of worry (Thomas Borkovec and colleagues, 2004) holds that worry is a verbal way of avoiding vivid, frightening images and the bodily arousal that goes with them; because worry dampens that arousal, it is negatively reinforced and continues. Treatment therefore has two arms. Relaxation (progressive muscle relaxation, or the applied relaxation of Lars-Goran Ost, 1987) brings down chronic tension and arousal. Cognitive therapy teaches the person to catch worry, test the predicted catastrophes, face worry images instead of avoiding them, and tolerate uncertainty. Example: Riya, a hostel student, lies awake worrying about exams, money and her parents' health. She learns to relax her shoulders on cue and to write down, test and challenge each \"what if\". Trials show such treatment works and, unlike drugs, its gains last after therapy ends. Option a is right because it is the psychological approach with lasting benefit in GAD. Exam trap: electroconvulsive therapy (option c) belongs to severe depression, and psychoanalytic therapy (option d) has little controlled evidence in GAD, so neither should tempt. Key note: pharmacotherapy (option b) is also helpful in GAD, so b is not false; relaxation plus CBT is keyed as the psychological treatment with lasting gains; a stands.", "topic": "Treatment of generalised anxiety disorder", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Addition, subtraction, multiplication and division are not possible in _____ measurement scale.", "options": {"a": "Nominal", "b": "Ordinal", "c": "Interval", "d": "Ratio"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: on a nominal scale the numbers are only labels, so addition, subtraction, multiplication and division mean nothing. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) sorted measurement into four levels, each adding one property. A nominal scale has identity only: numbers name categories with no order (jersey numbers, roll numbers, coding male as 1 and female as 2). You can only count how many cases fall in each category, find the mode and run chi-square. An ordinal scale adds order (class ranks, ratings), but the gaps between ranks are not equal, so the median and Spearman's rank correlation fit. An interval scale adds equal units but has no true zero (Celsius temperature, IQ); adding and subtracting are meaningful, but ratios are not, so 40 degrees is not twice as hot as 20. A ratio scale adds a true zero (weight, height, reaction time), so all four operations work and 60 kg really is twice 30 kg. Example: Arjun wears shirt number 10 and Kabir number 5. Adding them gives 15 and dividing gives 2, but neither result says anything about the two players. Option a is right because the nominal scale is the only level at which no arithmetic at all, not even ordering, is meaningful. Exam trap: interval tempts because ratios fail there, but addition and subtraction do work on interval data; the stem asks for the scale on which none of the four operations is possible. Key note: ordinal numbers do not support arithmetic either, but nominal is the scale where no arithmetic (not even ordering) is meaningful, the textbook answer; a stands.", "topic": "Levels of measurement", "fig": "Stanley Smith Stevens"}, {"q": "In which type of interview the topics and issues to be covered are specified in advance, however, the sequence and wording of the question in the course of the interview is decided by the interviewer?", "options": {"a": "Informal conversational interview", "b": "Interview guide approach", "c": "Standardised open-ended interview", "d": "Closed, fixed response interview"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: in the interview guide approach the topics and issues are listed in advance in outline form, but the interviewer decides the order and wording of questions as the interview unfolds. Michael Quinn Patton (1980), in Qualitative Evaluation Methods, described three approaches to qualitative interviewing and, in a summary table, set them beside the closed, fixed-response interview of quantitative work, running from least to most structured. In the informal conversational interview, questions arise naturally from the immediate context, with no set topics or wording. In the interview guide approach, a checklist of topics makes sure every respondent is asked about the same areas, while the interviewer keeps the conversation natural. In the standardised open-ended interview, the exact wording and sequence of every question are fixed in advance, but the answers are open. In the closed, fixed-response interview, both questions and answer categories are fixed, and the respondent picks from them. Each step along the line buys comparability and easier analysis at the price of flexibility and depth. The interview guide approach is close to what clinicians and survey researchers call a semi-structured interview. Example: Riya studies homesickness among first-year hostel students. Her guide lists family contact, food, friendships and sleep; with one student she starts with food, with another with friendships, phrasing each question in the student's own words. Option b is right because it is the only type with fixed topics but flexible sequence and wording. Exam trap: the standardised open-ended interview also uses open questions, so students confuse the two; the difference is that it fixes the exact wording and order, which this stem says the interviewer decides.", "topic": "Patton's qualitative interview types", "fig": "Michael Quinn Patton"}, {"q": "Which among the following is not the measure of central tendency?", "options": {"a": "Arithmetic Mean", "b": "Mode", "c": "Median", "d": "Mean Deviation"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: mean deviation is a measure of dispersion (variability), not of central tendency. A measure of central tendency gives one typical value that represents a whole set of scores. There are three main ones. The arithmetic mean is the sum of the scores divided by their number; it uses every score but is pulled by extreme values. The median is the middle score when the scores are put in order; it resists outliers and suits skewed or ordinal data. The mode is the most frequent score and the only one usable with nominal data. Mean deviation answers a different question: how far, on average, do the scores lie from the centre? It is the average of the absolute deviations of the scores from the mean (or median), with signs ignored. Example: Meera's three test scores are 2, 4 and 6. The mean is 4 and the median is 4. The deviations from the mean are 2, 0 and 2, so the mean deviation is 4 divided by 3, about 1.33 marks. The mean and median locate the centre; the mean deviation describes the spread around it. Option d is right because mean deviation describes scatter, not a typical value. Exam trap: mean deviation is the favourite decoy precisely because it contains the word \"mean\"; range, quartile deviation, standard deviation and variance are the other measures of spread that can appear in this slot.", "topic": "Central tendency versus dispersion", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The degree to which numerical data tend to spread about an average value is known as:", "options": {"a": "Range", "b": "Dispersion", "c": "Standard deviation", "d": "Standard error"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: dispersion is the general name for the degree to which scores spread out around an average value. It is also called variability or scatter. Two groups can share the same average and still differ sharply in how bunched or spread out their scores are, so a good description of data needs both a measure of central tendency and a measure of dispersion. Example: Riya's class and Arjun's class both have a mean of 60 marks in psychology. In Riya's class every student scored between 55 and 65; in Arjun's, scores ran from 20 to 95. The averages match, but dispersion is far greater in Arjun's class, and a teacher would plan very differently for each. Dispersion is measured in several ways. The range is the highest score minus the lowest. The quartile deviation is half the distance between the first and third quartiles. The mean deviation is the average absolute deviation from the mean. The variance is the average squared deviation from the mean, and the standard deviation is its square root, the most widely used measure. Option b is right because the stem defines the concept itself, while every other option is either one tool for measuring it or a different statistic altogether. Exam trap: standard deviation is so closely tied to spread that students pick it, but the stem asks for the name of the property, and standard deviation is only its best-known measure.", "topic": "Dispersion", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following curves is known as a normal probability curve?", "options": {"a": "Lepto Kurtic", "b": "Meso Kurtic", "c": "Platy Kurtic", "d": "Normo Kurtic"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the normal probability curve is mesokurtic. Kurtosis describes how peaked or flat a distribution is compared with the normal curve, and how heavy its tails are. Karl Pearson (1905) introduced the three terms, from the Greek kurtos, \"curved\" or \"bulging\". A mesokurtic curve (meso, \"middle\") has the moderate peakedness of the normal curve. A leptokurtic curve (lepto, \"thin\") is taller and narrower, with scores packed around the mean and heavier tails. A platykurtic curve (platy, \"flat\") is flatter and broader, with scores spread more evenly. On the moment measure the normal curve has a kurtosis of 3; software such as SPSS reports excess kurtosis (kurtosis minus 3), so a mesokurtic curve scores 0. Indian statistics texts also use the percentile coefficient, Ku = Q divided by (P90 minus P10), which is 0.263 for the normal curve; a larger value means platykurtic, a smaller one leptokurtic. The normal curve itself, derived by Abraham de Moivre (1733), is bell-shaped, symmetrical and unimodal, with mean, median and mode equal. Example: if most of Kabir's classmates score very close to the class average, with only a few far out, the curve is leptokurtic; if scores spread fairly evenly from low to high, it is platykurtic. Option b is right because mesokurtic means normal peakedness, the defining shape of the normal probability curve. Exam trap: \"Normo kurtic\" sounds like \"normal\" and is planted to catch guessers, but it is not a statistical term.", "topic": "Kurtosis and the normal curve", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The formula for converting raw score into Z score is:", "options": {"a": "Z = (M − X)/σ", "b": "Z = (M − σ)/X", "c": "Z = (σ − M)/X", "d": "Z = (X − M)/σ"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: a z score is found by subtracting the mean from the raw score and dividing by the standard deviation, Z = (X − M)/σ. A z score (standard score) tells you how many standard deviations a raw score lies above or below the mean of its group. The subtraction X minus M gives the distance from the mean in raw units and keeps the sign: positive if the score is above the mean, negative if below. Dividing by σ turns that distance into standard deviation units, so scores from different tests become comparable. A full set of z scores always has a mean of 0 and a standard deviation of 1. Example: Riya scores 70 in a psychology test where the class mean is 60 and the SD is 5, so z = (70 minus 60) divided by 5 = 2; she is two SDs above the mean, ahead of about 98 percent of a normal group. Kabir scores 55, so his z is minus 1, one SD below the mean. If Riya also scores 80 in statistics, where the mean is 75 and the SD 10, her z there is only 0.5, so psychology is her relatively stronger subject even though the raw mark is lower. Option d is right because it is the only formula that measures distance from the mean in SD units with the correct sign. Exam trap: option a uses the same parts with the subtraction reversed; it gives the right size but the wrong sign, so a score above the mean would come out negative. Derived scores such as T = 50 + 10z are built on this formula.", "topic": "z score formula", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Measurement error can be eliminated/reduced by:", "options": {"a": "Using parametric tests", "b": "Using a non-parametric test", "c": "Taking a large sample", "d": "Estimating standard error of deviation"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: taking a large sample reduces error, because random errors tend to cancel out as more observations are added. Every observed score or sample statistic contains some error. Random error pushes a result up on one occasion and down on another, with no fixed direction, so when many observations are averaged the ups and downs offset each other. In sampling, this is why the standard error of the mean equals the SD divided by the square root of N: quadrupling the sample halves the error. In testing, the same logic explains why adding items or observations raises reliability (the Spearman-Brown formula of Charles Spearman and William Brown, 1910). Example: Meera wants the average reaction time of college students. If she tests 10 students, one unusually fast or slow person can shift her mean a lot; with 400 students such chance quirks average out, and her estimate sits much closer to the true value. What a big sample cannot fix is systematic error (bias). In 1936 the Literary Digest magazine collected more than 2 million straw-poll ballots and still predicted the wrong winner of the United States presidential election, because its mailing lists (telephone directories, car registrations, its own subscribers) and its self-selected repliers did not represent the voters. Option c is right because sample size is the only option that actually lowers error. Exam trap: standard error sounds like a remedy, but it estimates how large the error is; it does not reduce it. Key note: strictly, a large sample reduces sampling error, and random measurement error averages out but systematic error does not; c is still the only option that reduces error; c stands.", "topic": "Reducing error with sample size", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A statistical test that does not specify conditions of normality scores and do not need to have well-defined population is:", "options": {"a": "Parametric test", "b": "Non-parametric test", "c": "Random test", "d": "Non-probability test"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: a non-parametric test makes no assumption that scores are normally distributed and does not need well-defined population parameters. Tests fall into two families. A parametric test (the t-test, ANOVA, Pearson's r) estimates population parameters such as the mean and variance, and assumes that scores come from a normal population, that groups have similar variances and that data are on an interval or ratio scale. A non-parametric test, also called a distribution-free test, works on ranks, signs or category counts instead, so it suits nominal or ordinal data, small samples and clearly skewed data. Sidney Siegel (1956) popularised these tests in psychology with his book Nonparametric Statistics for the Behavioral Sciences. Common examples are chi-square (frequencies), the Mann-Whitney U test (two independent groups), the Wilcoxon signed-rank test (two related samples), the Kruskal-Wallis test (three or more groups) and Spearman's rank correlation. Example: Kabir has only eight children rank a set of cartoons from most to least liked. Ranks are ordinal and eight is too few to check normality, so he uses Spearman's rho rather than Pearson's r. The price of this freedom is lower power when the data really are normal. Option b is right because freedom from the normality assumption and from fixed population parameters is the defining feature of non-parametric tests. Exam trap: \"non-probability\" sounds like \"non-parametric\", but non-probability describes sampling methods, not statistical tests.", "topic": "Non-parametric tests", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The MMPI-I consists of _____ validity scales and _____ clinical scales.", "options": {"a": "7, 12", "b": "5, 12", "c": "4, 9", "d": "3, 10"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: the original MMPI has 3 validity scales and 10 clinical scales. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory was built by Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) through empirical criterion keying: an item was kept if psychiatric patient groups answered it differently from normal people, whatever the item seemed to be about. It has 566 true-false items. The 10 clinical scales are 1 Hypochondriasis (Hs), 2 Depression (D), 3 Hysteria (Hy), 4 Psychopathic deviate (Pd), 5 Masculinity-femininity (Mf), 6 Paranoia (Pa), 7 Psychasthenia (Pt), 8 Schizophrenia (Sc), 9 Hypomania (Ma) and 0 Social introversion (Si). The 3 validity scales check test-taking attitude. L (Lie) catches naive attempts to look good by denying common small faults. F (Infrequency) picks up rare, odd answers that suggest random responding, faking bad or severe disturbance. K (Correction), added in 1946 by Paul Meehl and Starke Hathaway, picks up subtler defensiveness and is used to correct some clinical scales. The Cannot Say (?) score, the number of items left blank, is also checked. The MMPI-2 (1989) has 567 items and added validity scales such as VRIN, TRIN and F(B), while keeping the 10 clinical scales. Option d is right because 3 validity and 10 clinical scales is the standard count for the original inventory. Exam trap: the later versions have many more validity scales, and the MMPI-2-RF has 9 Restructured Clinical scales, so larger or odd numbers can look familiar; the stem asks about the first MMPI. Key note: counting Cannot Say gives 4 validity indicators, but option c pairs that with 9 clinical scales, which is wrong; 3 and 10 is the standard count; d stands.", "topic": "MMPI validity and clinical scales", "fig": ""}, {"q": "_____ validity is used for the purpose of determining whether the test score of people in one category are significantly different from those of people in other categories.", "options": {"a": "Criterion", "b": "Predictive", "c": "Content", "d": "Concurrent"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: concurrent validity is shown when a test separates people who already belong to different categories, such as patients and non-patients, at the time of testing. Validity asks whether a test measures what it claims to measure. In criterion-related validity, test scores are compared with an outside measure of the same quality, called the criterion. There are two kinds, and they differ only in timing. In concurrent validity the criterion is available now: the test is given alongside a present diagnosis, an established test or a current group membership. In predictive validity the criterion comes later, as when entrance scores are compared with marks at the end of the first year. Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955) defined concurrent validity as test and criterion measured at essentially the same time, named psychiatric diagnosis as a typical present criterion, and called the original MMPI's separation of patient groups from \"normals\" concurrent validity: if people in one category score significantly differently from people in another, the test reflects their present status. Example: Arjun builds a new depression questionnaire and gives it to 50 inpatients already diagnosed with depression and 50 college volunteers. The patients score much higher on the same day, so the questionnaire shows concurrent validity. Option d is right because the categories exist at the time of testing, which is the mark of concurrent validity. Exam trap: \"criterion\" (option a) is the parent category and so looks safe, but the stem asks for the specific type, and the comparison is made now, not against a future outcome. Key note: Cronbach and Meehl, and modern texts, also treat known-groups (contrasted groups) differences as evidence of construct validity, and \"criterion\" is the parent category of concurrent validity; the specific name for comparing present categories is concurrent validity; d stands.", "topic": "Concurrent validity and contrasted groups", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which among the following is not the method for estimating the reliability of tests?", "options": {"a": "Test-retest", "b": "Parallel forms", "c": "Split-half", "d": "Pair comparison"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: pair (paired) comparison is a scaling method, not a way of estimating the reliability of a test. Reliability is the consistency of test scores. In the classical test theory of Charles Spearman (1904), each observed score is a true score plus random error, and several methods estimate how much error a test carries. Test-retest gives the same test to the same people twice and correlates the two sets of scores, a coefficient of stability. Parallel (alternate) forms give two equivalent versions of the test, a coefficient of equivalence. Split-half divides one test into two halves (often odd and even items), correlates them, and steps the result up with the Spearman-Brown formula (Charles Spearman and William Brown, 1910), since each half is only half as long. Internal consistency formulas (G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson, 1937; Lee Cronbach's alpha, 1951) and inter-rater agreement complete the list. Paired comparison does something different: each object is paired with every other object, the judge picks one from each pair, and Louis Thurstone's (1927) law of comparative judgement turns the choices into scale values. With n objects there are n(n minus 1)/2 pairs. Example: Meera orders five job applicants by comparing each pair, making 10 comparisons; this ranks the applicants but says nothing about whether a test gives consistent scores. Option d is right because it is the only option that scales objects rather than checking score consistency. Exam trap: \"pair comparison\" sounds like correlating paired sets of scores, which is what reliability methods do, so it slips past a hurried reader.", "topic": "Methods of estimating reliability", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The first edition of the Binet-Simon intelligence scale was published in:", "options": {"a": "1903", "b": "1905", "c": "1908", "d": "1906"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the first Binet-Simon intelligence scale was published in 1905. In 1904 the French Ministry of Public Instruction set up a commission to find a way of identifying Paris schoolchildren who could not cope with ordinary lessons and needed special education. Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905), a psychologist and a physician, answered with 30 short tasks arranged in order of difficulty, from following a lit match with the eyes to defining abstract words. Unlike the sensory and reaction-time tests of Francis Galton (1884), they sampled higher mental processes such as judgement, comprehension and reasoning, which Binet saw as the core of intelligence. The 1908 revision grouped the items by the age at which most children passed them, creating the first age scale and the idea of mental age. The 1911 revision, the year Binet died, extended the scale to adults. William Stern (1912) proposed dividing mental age by chronological age, and Lewis Terman's Stanford-Binet (1916) multiplied this by 100 to give the intelligence quotient. Option b is right because 1905 is the date of the first edition. Exam trap: 1908 is the most tempting wrong date, because that revision introduced mental age, which many students remember as the scale's starting point; 1903 is the year of Binet's earlier book on intelligence, based on his two daughters.", "topic": "Binet-Simon scale history", "fig": "Alfred Binet and Théodore Simon"}, {"q": "In OCD compulsion are generally thought to be:", "options": {"a": "Repetitive thoughts of harming or distressing others", "b": "Overwhelming desirous to behave in an inappropriate fashion", "c": "The repetitive or ritualized pattern to prevent a negative outcome", "d": "Ritualized worrying about negative outcome of events"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: compulsions are repetitive or ritualised behaviours, or mental acts, that a person feels driven to perform to prevent a feared outcome or to reduce anxiety. Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) has two parts. Obsessions are intrusive, unwanted thoughts, urges or images (of contamination, harm, doubt, or forbidden sexual or religious themes) that cause marked anxiety. Compulsions are the responses: visible acts such as washing, checking, ordering and tapping, or hidden mental acts such as counting, praying or repeating words silently. DSM-5 says they are done in response to an obsession or according to rigid rules, aimed at preventing or reducing distress or some dreaded event, yet they are either not realistically connected to that event or clearly excessive. The relief they bring is brief, so they are negatively reinforced and grow stronger. Diagnosis needs the symptoms to be time-consuming (more than an hour a day) or to cause marked distress or impairment. Example: Kabir has the obsession that his flat will catch fire if the gas is left on. He checks the knob 20 times before leaving, counting aloud, and feels he must start again if interrupted. The checking is the compulsion; the fear of fire is the obsession. Option c is right because it captures the repetitive, ritualised act and its purpose of preventing a negative outcome. Exam trap: option a describes harm obsessions, so students who mix up the two halves of OCD pick it; obsessions are thoughts that arrive, compulsions are what the person does to neutralise them.", "topic": "Compulsions in OCD", "fig": ""}, {"q": "One of the two key steps of most basic form of the experimental method involves:", "options": {"a": "The presence or strength of some variable believed to affect behaviour", "b": "The effect of alterations are not carefully measured", "c": "All research participants have an equal chance of being exposed to the independent variable", "d": "Variables are measured to determine if they are related in any way"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: one of the two key steps of the basic experimental method is that the presence or strength of some variable believed to affect behaviour is systematically altered. In the experimental method, as Robert Baron and Donn Byrne describe it in their social psychology textbook, the researcher does two things. First, the presence or strength of some factor thought to influence behaviour is deliberately changed; this factor is the independent variable (IV). Second, the effects of these changes, if any, are carefully measured; the measured behaviour is the dependent variable (DV). If the DV shifts when only the IV has been changed, the researcher can conclude that the IV caused the shift. Two further conditions make that conclusion safe: random assignment of participants to conditions, so that the groups start out equal, and holding every other factor constant. For example, Meera plays background music to one group of students and keeps another in silence (step 1, altering the IV), then counts the sums each student solves correctly (step 2, measuring the DV). Option a is right because it names the first step, the systematic alteration of a variable believed to affect behaviour; the printed option is cut short before the words 'is systematically altered', but it is plainly step 1. Exam trap: random assignment (option c) is essential to a valid experiment, so examiners offer it as a step, but it is a condition for interpreting the results, not one of the two steps; and measuring variables to see whether they are related (option d) is the correlational method, which alters nothing.", "topic": "Experimental method: two steps", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Procedure in which the researchers who have contacts with participants do not know the hypothesis under investigation is known as:", "options": {"a": "Experimenter effect", "b": "Double-Blind procedure", "c": "Systematic observation", "d": "Co-relational research"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: a procedure in which the researchers who have contact with participants do not know the hypothesis under investigation is called a double-blind procedure. Researchers' expectations can leak into a study without anyone intending it. Robert Rosenthal (1966) showed that experimenters who expect a particular result can subtly bring it about, through tone of voice, smiles or the way they record answers; this is the experimenter expectancy effect. Participants, too, pick up cues about what a study is meant to show and behave accordingly, which Martin Orne (1962) called demand characteristics. In a single-blind study only the participants are kept unaware of their condition or of the hypothesis. In a double-blind study the people who run the sessions and collect the data are kept unaware as well, often by having a colleague who does not know the hypothesis or the group assignments test the participants. Drug trials are the classic case: neither the patient nor the doctor knows who received the active pill and who the placebo. For example, Arjun tests whether a herbal tea improves memory; his research assistant, who does not know which cups contain the tea or what Arjun predicts, gives the memory test, so her expectations cannot shape the scores. Option b is right because keeping the researchers in contact with participants ignorant of the hypothesis is exactly what double-blind means, and it guards against both expectancy effects and demand characteristics. Exam trap: option a, the experimenter effect, is the bias that the double-blind procedure is designed to prevent, not the procedure itself; examiners swap the problem for its remedy.", "topic": "Double-blind procedure", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following perspective focuses on the primacy of social processes and relativity?", "options": {"a": "Social and cultural", "b": "Evolutionary", "c": "Biological", "d": "Constructionist"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: the constructionist (social constructionist) perspective focuses on the primacy of social processes and on relativity. Social constructionism holds that what we take to be knowledge, including ideas such as mental illness or adolescence, is not simply discovered in nature but built up through social interaction, language and shared practices. Two consequences follow. First, social processes come first (primacy): conversations, institutions and relations of power create the meanings we live by. Second, knowledge is relative: because it is made by people in a particular culture and historical period, there is no single fixed truth, and the same behaviour can be understood quite differently in another place or time. Peter Berger and Thomas Luckmann set out the idea in 'The Social Construction of Reality' (1966), and Kenneth Gergen (1985) brought it into psychology, arguing that psychological knowledge is itself historically and culturally situated. For example, whether Kabir's restless, outspoken behaviour is seen as spirited, naughty or a disorder depends on the shared meanings of his family, school and era, not on a fact waiting to be found. Option d is right because only the constructionist perspective makes social processes primary and treats knowledge as relative. Exam trap: the social and cultural perspective (option a) also stresses society and culture, so it looks close, but it treats behaviour as shaped by real cultural influences rather than treating knowledge itself as socially built and relative. Key note: the sociocultural perspective does emphasise social processes, so a has some pull; but the pairing of primacy of social processes with relativity is the hallmark of the constructionist perspective, and d stands.", "topic": "Social constructionist perspective", "fig": "Kenneth Gergen"}, {"q": "Confounding variables is:", "options": {"a": "The unintended effect caused by the researcher on participants behaviour", "b": "Variables that researcher fails to eliminate, thus damaging the internal validity of an experiment", "c": "When two or more variables are measured to determine the relation", "d": "Both (B) and (C)"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: confounding variables are variables that the researcher fails to eliminate or control, so that they damage the internal validity of an experiment. In an experiment the researcher changes the independent variable (IV) and measures the dependent variable (DV), hoping that any change in the DV is caused by the IV alone. An extraneous variable is any other factor that could affect the DV. When such a factor happens to vary along with the IV, it becomes a confounding variable: the two are tangled together, and the researcher can no longer tell which of them produced the result. Internal validity is the confidence that the IV, and nothing else, caused the change; Donald Campbell and Julian Stanley (1963) listed the classic threats to it, such as history, maturation, testing and selection. Confounds are removed by random assignment, holding conditions constant, counterbalancing and matching. For example, Riya tests a new study method by teaching it to a morning class and comparing that class with an afternoon class taught the old way. If the morning class does better, she cannot tell whether the method or the time of day (fresher students) caused it: time of day is confounded with the method. Option b is right because it gives both the defining feature (an uncontrolled variable) and its consequence (loss of internal validity). Exam trap: option a describes the experimenter effect, one specific source of bias that can act as a confound but is not the definition of one; and option c describes correlational research, so 'both (B) and (C)' fails.", "topic": "Confounding variables", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Law of closure is:", "options": {"a": "The tendency of perceiving objects as whole entities, despite the fact that some parts may be missing", "b": "The tendency to perceive complex patterns in terms of simpler shapes", "c": "The tendency to perceive items located together as a group", "d": "The tendency to perceive stimuli as a part of continuous pattern"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: the law of closure is the tendency to perceive objects as whole entities even though some parts may be missing. Closure is one of the Gestalt laws of perceptual organisation. Max Wertheimer (1923) set out these grouping laws, and Wolfgang Kohler (1929) and Kurt Koffka (1935) developed them; the Gestalt school's central claim is that the whole is different from the sum of its parts, because the visual system actively organises what it receives. By closure, the mind fills gaps to complete a familiar shape: a circle drawn with small breaks is still seen as a circle, a logo made of separate strokes is read as one picture, and a partly hidden word on a signboard is still read correctly. For example, Meera sees a dotted outline of a triangle in her notebook and at once sees a triangle, not three separate broken lines. The other main laws are proximity (things close together are grouped), similarity (things that look alike are grouped), continuity (elements on a smooth line or curve belong together), common fate (things moving together are grouped) and the master law of Prägnanz (we see the simplest, most stable organisation the stimulus allows). Option a is right because filling in missing parts to see a complete whole is the definition of closure. Exam trap: option b, seeing complex patterns in terms of simpler shapes, is the law of Prägnanz (simplicity or good figure), the overarching Gestalt principle that is often confused with closure; closure is specifically about completing gaps.", "topic": "Gestalt law of closure", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A type of conditioning in which the presentation of the UCS precedes the presentations of the CS is known as:", "options": {"a": "Reconditioning", "b": "Simulation conditioning", "c": "Trace conditioning", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: none of these, because an arrangement in which the unconditioned stimulus (UCS) comes before the conditioned stimulus (CS) is backward conditioning, and that name is not among the options. In Ivan Pavlov's (1927) classical conditioning, the timing of the CS (a bell) and the UCS (food) decides how well learning occurs. In delay conditioning the CS begins first and is still on when the UCS arrives; this is the most effective arrangement, because the CS works as a warning signal. In trace conditioning the CS starts and ends before the UCS, leaving a gap, so the animal must hold a memory trace of the CS; it is weaker. In simultaneous conditioning the CS and UCS begin together, and learning is poor. In backward conditioning the UCS comes first and the CS follows. Because the CS then predicts nothing (the important event has already happened), backward conditioning produces little or no conditioned response, and the CS may even come to signal that the UCS will not occur (inhibitory conditioning). For example, if Kabir's dog is given food and only then hears a bell, the bell does not come to make the dog salivate. Option d is right because none of the three named procedures puts the UCS first. Exam trap: 'simulation conditioning' is probably a misprint for simultaneous conditioning, but even that is not UCS first, since the two stimuli start together; and trace conditioning is a forward arrangement in which the CS comes first.", "topic": "Backward conditioning", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The production process is an important factor in:", "options": {"a": "Fixed-interval schedule", "b": "Observational learning", "c": "Chaining", "d": "Shaping"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the production process is an important factor in observational learning. Albert Bandura's social learning theory (1977) explains how people learn by watching others (models). In the Bobo doll study of Albert Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross (1961), children who watched an adult punch and kick an inflatable doll later copied the same acts. Bandura named four processes that observational learning needs. Attention: the observer must notice the model and the relevant features of the act. Retention: the act is stored in memory as images and verbal descriptions. Production (motor reproduction): the stored memory is converted into actual behaviour, which needs the physical ability, and the act is refined through practice and feedback. Motivation: the observer needs a reason to perform, such as direct, vicarious or self-reinforcement. Motivation decides whether a learned act is actually performed, which is why Bandura separated learning from performance. For example, Arjun watches a video of a cricketer playing a cover drive, attends to the footwork and remembers the steps, but cannot play the shot until his body can carry out the movement; that third step is the production process. Option b is right because production is one of Bandura's four processes of observational learning. Exam trap: chaining and shaping (options c and d) both build up a behaviour step by step and so sound like producing a response, but they are operant techniques from B. F. Skinner's (1953) work, driven by reinforcement, not stages of learning by watching a model.", "topic": "Bandura's observational learning", "fig": "Albert Bandura"}, {"q": "The aspects of our physical states that serve as retrieval cues for information stored in long term memory is referred to as:", "options": {"a": "Context-dependent memory", "b": "Encoding specificity principle", "c": "Procedural memory", "d": "State-dependent retrieval"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: the aspects of our physical (internal) states that serve as retrieval cues for information in long-term memory are referred to as state-dependent retrieval. Memories are stored together with the cues present at encoding, and recall improves when those cues are present again. By the encoding specificity principle of Endel Tulving and Donald Thomson (1973), a retrieval cue works to the extent that it was encoded along with the memory. Two kinds of cue are distinguished. In context-dependent memory the external setting is the cue: Duncan Godden and Alan Baddeley (1975) had divers learn words underwater or on the beach, and recall was best in the same place. In state-dependent memory the internal physical or mental state is the cue: Donald Goodwin and colleagues (1969) found that material learned while intoxicated was recalled better when intoxicated again, and Gordon Bower (1981) extended the idea to mood-dependent memory. For example, Riya revises late at night after two cups of strong coffee; she may recall more in the test if she is in the same caffeinated, aroused state, because her bodily state at retrieval matches her state at learning. Option d is right because the stem names our physical states as the cues, which defines state-dependent retrieval. Exam trap: context means outside (the room, the place) and state means inside (drugs, arousal, mood); option a is the external version, and option b is the broad principle behind both rather than the name for internal-state cues.", "topic": "State-dependent retrieval", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Yerkes Dodson's law states:", "options": {"a": "Human seeks an optimal level of arousal not minimal level of arousal", "b": "Behaviour is pulled by the expectation of desirable outcomes", "c": "The level of arousal beyond which performance begins to decline is a function of task difficulty", "d": "Behaviour is pushed from within by drives"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the Yerkes-Dodson law states that the level of arousal beyond which performance begins to decline is a function of task difficulty. Robert Yerkes and John Dodson (1908) trained mice to choose between a light and a dark box, using electric shocks of different strengths for wrong choices. On the medium and hard discriminations learning was fastest with moderate shocks (on the easy one, the stronger the shock, the faster the learning), and the harder the discrimination, the weaker the shock that gave the best learning. The law as later taught says performance is an inverted-U function of arousal: too little arousal leaves a person drowsy and careless, a moderate amount gives the best performance, and too much makes the person tense and disorganised. The peak (the optimum) is not the same for every task: it is relatively high for simple or well-learned tasks and lower for difficult or new ones. For example, Kabir's exam-day nerves help him run fast in a school race (a simple task) but make his mind go blank on a difficult maths paper (a complex task). Option c is right because it states the distinctive claim of the law: the point at which more arousal starts to hurt depends on how hard the task is. Exam trap: option a, seeking an optimal rather than a minimal level of arousal, is optimal arousal theory (Donald Hebb, 1955); it shares the idea that moderate arousal is best, but it is a theory of motivation, not the law linking arousal, performance and task difficulty.", "topic": "Yerkes-Dodson law", "fig": "Robert Yerkes and John Dodson"}, {"q": "Sternberg's Triarchic theory of intelligence proposes _____ as the basic type of intelligence.", "options": {"a": "Crystallised, fluid and componential", "b": "Technological, integral and practical", "c": "Bodily-kinesthetic, musical and personal", "d": "Componential, practical and creative"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: Robert Sternberg's triarchic theory proposes componential, practical and creative intelligence as the basic types. In 'Beyond IQ' (1985) Robert Sternberg argued that IQ tests capture only part of intelligence and divided it into three parts (triarchic means ruled by three). Componential (analytical) intelligence covers the mental processes used in thinking: planning, monitoring, encoding and comparing information, the skills school tests reward. Experiential (creative) intelligence is the ability to deal with new situations and to make familiar tasks automatic, combining experiences in original ways. Contextual (practical) intelligence is street smarts: adapting to, shaping and selecting environments so as to succeed in everyday life. He later called the balanced use of analytical, creative and practical abilities successful intelligence. For example, Meera tops her written tests (componential), invents a new way to explain fractions to her younger brother (creative) and talks a shopkeeper into a fair price (practical). Option d is right because componential, practical and creative are exactly Sternberg's three intelligences, with creative standing for the experiential subtheory. Exam trap: option a slips one of Sternberg's terms (componential) in beside Raymond Cattell's fluid and crystallised intelligence (proposed 1943, tested 1963), so it looks half right; mixed lists like this are the usual bait.", "topic": "Sternberg's triarchic theory", "fig": "Robert Sternberg"}, {"q": "_____ is a hormone which is involved in the breakdown of fats. It is released when food reaches the part of the intestine immediately below the stomach.", "options": {"a": "Cholecystokinin", "b": "Estradiol", "c": "Antidiuretic hormone", "d": "Catecholamine"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: cholecystokinin (CCK) is the hormone involved in the breakdown of fats that is released when food reaches the duodenum, the part of the small intestine immediately below the stomach. When partly digested food, especially fat and protein, passes from the stomach into the duodenum, cells in the intestinal wall release CCK into the blood. CCK has two main digestive effects: it makes the gallbladder contract and release bile, which breaks fat into small droplets (emulsification), and it makes the pancreas release digestive enzymes such as lipase, which split fats into fatty acids. It also slows the emptying of the stomach. For psychologists CCK matters as a satiety signal: James Gibbs, R. C. Young and Gerard Smith (1973) injected CCK into hungry rats and found that they ate smaller meals, so it helps to end a meal. It works alongside other signals: ghrelin from the empty stomach starts hunger, and leptin from fat cells signals long-term energy stores. For example, after Arjun eats a rich paneer dish, CCK released from his duodenum helps digest the fat and adds to the full feeling that makes him stop eating. Option a is right because CCK is the duodenal hormone linked to fat digestion. Exam trap: catecholamine (option d) tempts because epinephrine and norepinephrine really do help break down stored body fat (lipolysis) in stress or fasting; but they come from the adrenal medulla and sympathetic nerves, and they are a class of chemicals, not the hormone released when food reaches the duodenum.", "topic": "Cholecystokinin (CCK)", "fig": ""}, {"q": "According to Cannon-Bard theory:", "options": {"a": "The bodily changes are much the same for many emotions even if there are differences in the patterns of responses", "b": "The emotions we feel result from our perception of bodily changes", "c": "Felt emotions and bodily changes occur in parallel with each other resulting from activity in brain areas", "d": "Felt emotions result from appraisal or evaluation of the information about the environmental situation"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: according to the Cannon-Bard theory, felt emotions and bodily changes occur in parallel with each other, both resulting from activity in brain areas. William James (1884) and Carl Lange (1885) had argued that the body reacts first and the felt emotion is our perception of those bodily changes: we feel afraid because we tremble. Walter Cannon (1927) attacked this view: emotion survives when the viscera are cut off from the brain, visceral changes are too slow and too similar across emotions to tell fear from anger, and injected adrenaline produces arousal without real emotion. Cannon's own alternative, which Philip Bard (1928) extended through experiments on rage in cats, was that an emotional stimulus is processed in the brain (Cannon pointed to the thalamus), which sends signals at the same time to the cortex, producing the conscious feeling, and to the body, producing arousal. Feeling and bodily change therefore happen together and independently; neither causes the other. Later research placed the key structures in the hypothalamus and limbic system rather than the thalamus, but the parallel idea remains. For example, when Riya sees a snake on the path, her fear and her racing heart arise at the same moment from the same brain signal. Option c is right because parallel feeling and arousal, both produced by brain activity, is the core claim of the theory. Exam trap: option a is something Cannon really said, so it feels familiar, but it was his criticism of the James-Lange theory, not the Cannon-Bard theory's own account. Key note: a is a true statement made by Cannon, but as his objection to James-Lange; the Cannon-Bard theory's own claim is c, and c stands.", "topic": "Cannon-Bard theory of emotion", "fig": "Walter Cannon and Philip Bard"}, {"q": "Libido is wholly intrapsychic. It attaches itself to the mental representation of objects satisfying instinctual needs. This process is called:", "options": {"a": "Psychic energy", "b": "Drive reduction", "c": "Cathexis", "d": "Catharsis"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: the process by which libido attaches itself to the mental representation of objects that satisfy instinctual needs is called cathexis. From his earliest writings in the 1890s, Sigmund Freud saw the mind as an energy system. The instincts supply psychic energy, and the energy of the life instincts is called libido. Libido is wholly intrapsychic: it does not flow into the real person or thing but into the inner image (mental representation) of it. Investing energy in such a representation is cathexis, the word James Strachey used to translate Freud's German Besetzung (occupation or charge). A hungry infant cathects the image of the breast; an adult may cathect a lover, a cause or a hobby. The ego and superego can oppose an id cathexis with an anticathexis, a counter-investment of energy that keeps a wish repressed, which is why repression is costly: energy spent holding a wish back is lost to work and love. For example, Kabir's libido is cathected to his idea of a famous cricketer, so thoughts of the cricketer feel charged and absorbing, and much of his attention flows towards them. Option c is right because cathexis names exactly the attaching of libido to an object's mental representation. Exam trap: catharsis (option d) looks and sounds like cathexis, but it is the discharge of pent-up emotion, as in the cathartic method of Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud (1895), not the binding of energy to an object.", "topic": "Cathexis in Freud's theory", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "Introjection is:", "options": {"a": "Unconsciously incorporating someone else's value into one's own personality", "b": "Repressing threatening beliefs or impulses", "c": "Using and believing superficially plausible explanations to justify illicit behaviour", "d": "Transferring behaviours from one object to another that is less threatening"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: introjection is unconsciously incorporating someone else's values into one's own personality. It is both a defence mechanism and a process of development in psychoanalytic theory. The term was introduced by Sandor Ferenczi (1909) and adopted by Sigmund Freud (1915), and Anna Freud (1936) listed it among the defences in 'The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence'. In introjection a person swallows whole the attitudes, standards or traits of another person, usually a powerful or loved one, so that they feel like one's own rather than borrowed. Children introject their parents' rules and prohibitions, and in Freud's account this is how the superego forms. It also eases anxiety: by taking in a feared person's standards, one feels less threatened by that person (Anna Freud's identification with the aggressor builds on it). Introjection is the opposite of projection, which pushes one's own unacceptable feelings out onto others. For example, Meera grew up hearing her father say that wasting food is shameful; as an adult she feels real guilt when she leaves food on her plate, as if the rule were her own idea. Option a is right because it defines introjection as the unconscious taking in of another person's values. Exam trap: introjection and projection sound like a pair and are often reversed; introjection brings others' qualities in, while projection pushes one's own impulses out.", "topic": "Introjection (defence mechanism)", "fig": ""}, {"q": "NEO-PI-R was developed by:", "options": {"a": "Costa and Costa (1984)", "b": "McCrae and Eysenck (1991)", "c": "Costa and McCrae (1992)", "d": "McCrae and John (1988)"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: the NEO-PI-R (Revised NEO Personality Inventory) was developed by Paul Costa and Robert McCrae (1992). Paul Costa and Robert McCrae worked at the US National Institute on Aging, studying how personality changes with age. Their first inventory measured three broad domains, Neuroticism, Extraversion and Openness to experience, which gave the name NEO. The NEO Personality Inventory of 1985 added Agreeableness and Conscientiousness, so that it covered all five factors of the Five-Factor Model (the Big Five). The 1992 revision, the NEO-PI-R, has 240 items answered on a five-point agree-disagree scale and scores five domains, each with six facets, giving 30 facets in all; Neuroticism, for instance, includes anxiety, angry hostility, depression, self-consciousness, impulsiveness and vulnerability. Scores are reported as T scores. A short 60-item NEO Five-Factor Inventory (NEO-FFI) had appeared in 1989, and the NEO-PI-3 (2005) followed. For example, Kabir's NEO-PI-R profile at a counselling centre shows high Conscientiousness, especially on self-discipline and achievement striving, and low Neuroticism. Option c is right because the NEO-PI-R and its 1992 date belong to Costa and McCrae. Exam trap: the other options pair real surnames with the wrong partner or date; Robert McCrae did write with Oliver John on the five-factor model, and Hans Eysenck built a rival model of personality, but neither partnership made the NEO-PI-R.", "topic": "NEO-PI-R authorship", "fig": "Paul Costa and Robert McCrae"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a projective test?", "options": {"a": "Word association test", "b": "Rorschach's ink blot test", "c": "Thematic apperception test", "d": "Sentence completion test"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: the word association test is the option set apart here as not a projective test, although strictly all four options are projective. Projective tests use ambiguous or open stimuli onto which people project their needs and conflicts. Francis Galton (1879) timed the associations that a list of words called up in his own mind, and Carl Jung (1910) made word association a clinical tool: 100 stimulus words, each reply timed, with complex indicators (a long delay, no reply, an odd answer) betraying an emotionally charged complex. Gardner Lindzey (1959) sorted projective techniques by the response they demand: association (word association and Hermann Rorschach's (1921) inkblots), construction (the Thematic Apperception Test of Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray, 1935), completion (sentence completion, such as Julian Rotter's Incomplete Sentences Blank, 1950), choice or ordering, and expression. Of the four options, word association is the most controlled: a fixed list, a one-word reply, and scoring that leans on reaction time. For example, when the tester says 'mother' and Riya pauses six seconds before saying 'kind', the delay is read as a complex indicator. Option a is right because word association is the most controlled, least open-ended of the four, so it is the one set apart. Exam trap: many pick sentence completion, whose stems steer the answer; but in theory questions, treat word association as Lindzey does, as a projective association technique. Key note: strictly all four options are projective (in Lindzey's scheme word association and the Rorschach are association techniques, the TAT a construction technique and sentence completion a completion technique), so the item is flawed. Word association is keyed because it is the answer this same item was given on the RML 2021 paper and it is the most controlled of the four (a single-word response to a fixed list, from Francis Galton's 1879 experiment and Carl Jung's diagnostic association studies), and some texts instead call sentence completion semi-projective; the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Projective test classification", "fig": ""}, {"q": "\"Anyone who either cannot lead the common life and therefore does not partake of the society is either a beast or God\": these famous lines are by:", "options": {"a": "Aristotle", "b": "Socrates", "c": "Plato", "d": "Comte"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: the lines about a beast or a god are by Aristotle. In 'Politics' (Book I, written about 350 BCE), Aristotle (384 to 322 BCE) argued that man is by nature a social animal (literally a political animal, a creature of the polis, the city-state). He added that anyone who cannot share in the common life, or is so self-sufficient as not to need it, is no part of the state and must be either a beast or a god. His point is that humans become fully human only in a community; to live outside it is to be less than human (a beast) or more than human (a god). Social psychology textbooks quote him to show that interest in human social nature is ancient, long before social psychology became a science in the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries. Aristotle also wrote on persuasion in the 'Rhetoric' (ethos, pathos and logos), which anticipates modern work on attitude change. For example, when Arjun moves to a new city and feels lost until he makes friends, his experience fits Aristotle's claim that people need a community to flourish. Option a is right because the beast-or-god passage comes from Aristotle's 'Politics'. Exam trap: Plato was Aristotle's teacher and Socrates was Plato's, so their names are offered as near-misses; Plato also wrote about the ideal society in the 'Republic', but the beast-or-god sentence is Aristotle's.", "topic": "Aristotle on human sociality", "fig": "Aristotle"}, {"q": "_____ is often credited with the first experiment in social psychology.", "options": {"a": "Ringelmann (1911)", "b": "McDougall (1908)", "c": "E.A. Ross (1908)", "d": "D. Norman Triplett (1898)"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: Norman Triplett (1898) is often credited with the first experiment in social psychology. Norman Triplett, working at Indiana University, studied cycling records and noticed that cyclists rode faster when paced by another rider or racing a rival than when riding alone against the clock. To test whether the presence of others speeds people up, he had children turn fishing reels as fast as they could, sometimes alone and sometimes beside another child doing the same task. Many wound faster in company, though some did worse. Published in 1898 in the American Journal of Psychology, the study began research on what Floyd Allport (1920) named social facilitation, later explained by Robert Zajonc (1965): the presence of others raises arousal, which helps on easy, well-learned tasks and hurts on hard or new ones. For example, Meera runs her best time when racing beside a classmate rather than alone. Option d is right because Triplett's reel-winding study is the standard first experiment in social psychology textbooks. Exam trap: William McDougall and Edward Alsworth Ross (both 1908) are firsts too, but for the first two textbooks titled social psychology, not for the first experiment; and Max Ringelmann's rope-pulling studies show effort falling in groups (social loafing), the opposite effect. Key note: some historians (David Kravitz and Barbara Martin, 1986) point out that Ringelmann's rope-pulling work was done in the 1880s, before Triplett's, though published only in 1913; but the standard answer credits Triplett, and d stands.", "topic": "First social psychology experiment", "fig": "Norman Triplett"}, {"q": "Hermeneutic is the:", "options": {"a": "Science of interpreting communication verbal, non-verbal, or textual", "b": "Of knowing how our memories influence our understanding of the world", "c": "The way our thought and feeling are affected by inputs from people", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: hermeneutics is the science (theory and method) of interpreting communication, whether verbal, non-verbal or textual. The word comes from the Greek hermeneuein, to interpret, and is linked with Hermes, the messenger who carried and explained the messages of the gods. Hermeneutics began as the art of interpreting sacred and legal texts. Friedrich Schleiermacher (1768 to 1834) widened it into a general theory of understanding any text or speech, and Wilhelm Dilthey (1883) made it the method of the human sciences: the natural sciences explain from outside, but the human sciences must understand (Verstehen) meanings from inside. Martin Heidegger (1927) and Hans-Georg Gadamer (1960) argued that all understanding is interpretation shaped by the interpreter's own background. A central idea is the hermeneutic circle: we understand the parts of a text through the whole and the whole through its parts. In psychology, hermeneutics underlies qualitative methods such as Jonathan Smith's (1996) interpretative phenomenological analysis, with its double hermeneutic: the researcher interprets the participant's own interpretation of an experience. For example, when Riya analyses interviews with new mothers, she reads each remark in the light of the whole interview, and the interview in the light of each remark. Option a is right because interpreting the meaning of communication is exactly what hermeneutics studies. Exam trap: options b and c borrow phrases from memory research and from the definition of social psychology, so they sound like textbook definitions, but neither is about interpreting meaning.", "topic": "Hermeneutics", "fig": ""}, {"q": "An experiment where the goal was to observe if the authority position would cause people to act differently was carried out by:", "options": {"a": "Stanley Milgram", "b": "Philip Zimbardo", "c": "Solomon Asch", "d": "Muzafer Sherif"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: the experiment whose goal was to see whether a position of authority would make people act differently was carried out by Philip Zimbardo, the Stanford Prison Experiment (1971). In August 1971 Philip Zimbardo turned a basement corridor of the psychology building at Stanford University into a mock prison. He picked 24 male college students who had tested as the most stable and healthy of about 75 volunteers, and a coin toss made each one a guard or a prisoner, so any difference in how they behaved could not come from their personalities. Guards got khaki uniforms, whistles, wooden batons and mirrored sunglasses; prisoners were picked up at home by real police, given smocks, numbers instead of names and a chain on one ankle. Zimbardo himself played the prison superintendent. Within a day the prisoners rebelled, and the guards answered with sleep loss, push-ups, solitary confinement in a closet and humiliation, some of it worse at night when they thought no one was watching. Several prisoners broke down and had to be released. The study was planned for two weeks but was stopped after six days, after Christina Maslach, who had just finished her Stanford PhD, objected to what she saw. Craig Haney, Curtis Banks and Zimbardo (1973) concluded that the situation and the roles, not the people, produced the cruelty; Zimbardo later called this the Lucifer Effect (2007). Later critics, such as Stephen Reicher and Alexander Haslam's BBC Prison Study (2006) and Thibault Le Texier (2018), argue the guards were coached to be tough, but the textbook reading is still that a position of authority changes how ordinary people act. For example, Rohan, a quiet hostel student, is made warden of the junior block and within weeks is shouting at juniors and inventing new rules; the position, not his personality, changed how he acts. Option b is right because the Stanford Prison Experiment put ordinary people into a position of authority (or powerlessness) to see whether the position itself would change their behaviour. Exam trap: Stanley Milgram's obedience study (1963) is also about authority, but it asked whether people would obey orders from someone in authority, not whether being put in a position of authority changes how you act. Key note: until 25 Nov 2026 this mock keyed a, Milgram, reading the stem as obeying an authority. The stem's 'authority position' is the position the participants themselves were placed in, which is Zimbardo's question, and the usual summary of his study is that when people assume positions of authority they act the role, so the key is now b. If an examiner meant obedience to an authority figure the answer would be Milgram, so learn both.", "topic": "Zimbardo's Stanford Prison Experiment", "fig": "Philip Zimbardo"}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"jammu-2020": [{"q": "Influential model of attitude change and formation is:", "concept": "The answer is d: the elaboration likelihood model (ELM) is the influential model of attitude change and formation. Richard Petty and John Cacioppo (1986) set it out after a run of studies from the early 1980s. Elaboration means thinking carefully about the arguments in a message, and the likelihood of elaboration depends on two things: motivation (the issue matters to you, or you enjoy thinking) and ability (time, knowledge, no distraction). When both are high, people take the central route: they weigh the quality of the arguments, and the attitude that results is strong, lasting, resistant to counter-attack and good at predicting behaviour. When either is low, they take the peripheral route: they lean on cues such as an attractive or famous source, the sheer number of arguments or their own good mood, and the change is weaker and shorter-lived. In the classic study by Richard Petty, John Cacioppo and Rachel Goldman (1981), students who thought a new exam policy would affect them personally were moved by strong arguments and not by weak ones, while students for whom it was irrelevant were swayed mainly by the prestige of the source. Example: Arjun, buying his first laptop, compares battery tests and benchmark scores (central route); his cousin, who does not care much, buys the one a cricket star advertises (peripheral route). Option d is right because it is the exact name of this two-route model of persuasion. Exam trap: the three wrong options are made-up blends of real words, and 'expectancy' and 'value' borrow from expectancy-value models of attitude structure; the only genuine rival dual-process model is Shelly Chaiken's (1980) heuristic-systematic model, which is not offered here.", "traps": {"a": "Value likelihood model is not a real model: it swaps 'elaboration' for 'value', a word from Martin Fishbein's expectancy-value approach, in which an attitude is the sum of beliefs about an object weighted by how much each attribute is valued.", "b": "Expectancy elaboration model is an invented blend; 'expectancy' echoes expectancy-value ideas behind the theory of reasoned action (Martin Fishbein and Icek Ajzen, 1975), which explains attitudes and intentions, not two routes of persuasion.", "c": "Value elaboration model is also invented; it keeps 'elaboration' but drops 'likelihood', the core idea that the chance of careful thought depends on motivation and ability."}}, {"q": "All of the following are dimensions of Kelley's attribution theory except:", "concept": "The answer is d: constancy is not one of the dimensions in Harold Kelley's attribution theory. Harold Kelley (1967) proposed the covariation model: to decide whether a behaviour was caused by the person (an internal attribution) or by the stimulus or situation (an external attribution), we check how the behaviour varies across three kinds of information. Consensus asks whether other people behave the same way towards the same stimulus. Consistency asks whether this person behaves this way towards this stimulus on other occasions. Distinctiveness asks whether the person behaves this way only towards this stimulus or towards many others too. Example: Kabir laughs loudly at a stand-up comic. If everyone in the hall laughs (high consensus), Kabir laughs at this comic every time (high consistency) and he rarely laughs at other comics (high distinctiveness), we decide the comic is genuinely funny: an external attribution. If nobody else laughs (low consensus), Kabir always laughs at this comic (high consistency) and he laughs at every comic (low distinctiveness), we decide it is something about Kabir: an internal attribution. When consistency is low, we put the behaviour down to passing circumstances. For one-off observations Kelley later added causal schemas such as discounting. Option d is right because constancy is a term from perception (size, shape and colour constancy: seeing objects as stable although their image on the retina changes), not a source of attributional information. Exam trap: examiners use 'constancy' because it sounds like 'consistency', and they also reverse definitions; consistency is the same person over time, distinctiveness is the same person across different stimuli, and consensus is different people with the same stimulus.", "traps": {"a": "Consensus is a genuine Kelley dimension: the degree to which other people react to the same stimulus in the same way; high consensus points to an external cause.", "b": "Consistency is a genuine Kelley dimension: the degree to which the person reacts to this stimulus in the same way on different occasions; low consistency points to temporary circumstances.", "c": "Distinctiveness is a genuine Kelley dimension: whether the person reacts this way only to this stimulus or to many others; high distinctiveness points to the stimulus as the cause."}}, {"q": "Dissonance can be described as:", "concept": "The answer is c: cognitive dissonance is both a motivational state and a state of arousal. Leon Festinger (1957) defined dissonance as the unpleasant tension we feel when two of our cognitions (beliefs, attitudes or knowledge of our own behaviour) clash, such as 'smoking kills' and 'I smoke'. Like hunger, this tension works as a drive: it motivates us to reduce it by changing a belief or behaviour, adding consonant thoughts ('my grandfather smoked and lived to ninety') or playing down the importance of the clash. In the classic study by Leon Festinger and J. Merrill Carlsmith (1959), students paid only one dollar to tell the next participant that a dull task was fun later rated the task as more enjoyable than students paid twenty dollars, because they had too little outside justification for the lie. Dissonance is also physiological arousal. Mark Zanna and Joel Cooper (1974) gave participants a placebo pill; those told the pill would make them tense could blame their arousal on it, and the usual attitude change disappeared. Robert Croyle and Joel Cooper (1983) then recorded raised skin conductance in people who freely wrote essays against their own views. Example: Meera, who prides herself on saving money, buys an expensive phone; she feels uneasy and keeps telling herself that the camera will save her hiring a photographer. Option c is right because dissonance is a drive-like motivational state and an arousal state at the same time. Exam trap: Daryl Bem's (1967) self-perception theory explained the same attitude-change results with no arousal at all, which is why some students think dissonance is purely cognitive; the pill and skin conductance studies showed that the arousal is real and drives the change.", "traps": {"a": "A motivational state alone is only half the picture: Leon Festinger's dissonance is a drive to reduce tension, but the misattribution study of Mark Zanna and Joel Cooper (1974) showed it is also arousal.", "b": "A state of arousal alone is also half the picture: the arousal matters because it pushes people to change attitudes or behaviour, which is the motivational side Festinger stressed.", "d": "Neither would fit only Daryl Bem's (1967) self-perception account, which treats attitude change as cool self-observation; the misattribution and skin conductance studies showed dissonance involves both drive and arousal."}}, {"q": "According to Jones and Davis correspondent inference theory, we find person's action to be most informative about variant dispositions when:", "concept": "The answer is b: by correspondent inference theory, an action tells us most about a person's stable dispositions when it is judged to be intentional. Edward Jones and Keith Davis (1965) asked when we can infer that an act 'corresponds' to an underlying trait, so that a hostile act reveals a hostile person. The first step is to decide that the act was intended: the actor knew what the consequences would be and had the ability to bring them about. Accidents tell us nothing about character. The inference then gets stronger when the act was freely chosen rather than forced by the situation, when it produces non-common effects (outcomes that only this choice brings, which pinpoint the motive), and when it is low in social desirability, because doing the expected thing (thanking a host) reveals little while out-of-role behaviour reveals a lot. Hedonic relevance (the act affects us) and personalism (it seems aimed at us) strengthen it further. Example: if Riya knocks her classmate's coffee over his notes on purpose, knowing what will happen, we infer hostility; if someone jogged her elbow, we learn nothing about her. Option b is right because judged intentionality is the gateway to a correspondent inference. Exam trap: 'consistent' belongs to Harold Kelley's (1967) covariation model (the same behaviour over time), not to Jones and Davis, so a 'both' option pairing intention with a consistent goal looks plausible but mixes two theories. Key note: the printed 'variant dispositions' is almost certainly a slip for 'invariant' (stable) dispositions, which is what the theory is about; since c is not a Jones and Davis condition, d falls with it, and the answer used here, b, stands.", "traps": {"a": "An action that depends on the person's situation (forced by role, orders or circumstances) is the least informative; Jones and Davis held that situationally constrained behaviour supports only a weak correspondent inference.", "c": "'Caused by a consistent goal' is not one of Jones and Davis's conditions; consistency over time is a dimension of Harold Kelley's (1967) covariation model.", "d": "Both (B) and (C) fails because c is not part of correspondent inference theory, so only b is correct."}}, {"q": "The reinforcement affect model predicts that:", "concept": "The answer is b: the reinforcement-affect model predicts that people will be attracted to someone they associate with a good feeling. Donn Byrne and Gerald Clore (1970) built this model on classical conditioning. Things that make us feel good (praise, a comfortable room, good news) act as rewards, and anyone who happens to be present gets linked with the pleasant feeling, so we like them, even if they did not cause it. Bad feelings work the other way. William Griffitt (1970) found that students rated an unseen stranger less favourably when they sat in an uncomfortably hot room than in a comfortable one, although the stranger had nothing to do with the heat. Example: Kabir is introduced to Anjali at the party where he hears that he has topped the exam; weeks later he still feels warmly towards her without knowing why. The same idea explains why a first meeting in a pleasant café tends to go better than one at a noisy, crowded bus stand. Option b is right because liking through association with positive feelings is the model's core prediction. Exam trap: the other three options are predictions of different theories from the same chapter on attraction. Options a and d come from the gain-loss study of Elliot Aronson and Darwyn Linder (1965), in which a confederate whose remarks about the participant moved from negative to positive was liked most, and one whose remarks moved from positive to negative was liked least. Option c is Fritz Heider's (1958) balance theory.", "traps": {"a": "Being liked more in the gain condition is the gain-loss effect of Elliot Aronson and Darwyn Linder (1965): a confederate whose evaluations change from negative to positive is liked more than one who is positive throughout; it concerns the sequence of evaluations, not association with a good feeling.", "c": "Discomfort at liking someone who disagrees with you is Fritz Heider's (1958) balance theory: you, the other person and the attitude topic form an unbalanced triad that you feel pressure to fix.", "d": "Being disliked more in the loss condition is the other half of Aronson and Linder's (1965) gain-loss effect; a confederate who turns from praise to criticism is liked least."}}, {"q": "Volunteer process model was given by:", "concept": "The answer is b: the volunteer process model was given by Allen Omoto and Mark Snyder. Allen Omoto and Mark Snyder (1995) studied people who volunteered to support those living with AIDS, often for months or years and with no obligation to do so. Their model treats volunteering as a process with three stages. Antecedents are what the person brings: personality (such as empathy and a helping disposition), motives, and life circumstances such as existing social support. Experiences are what happens during service: satisfaction with the work and integration into the organisation. Consequences are the outcomes: how long the person keeps serving, and changes in attitudes and knowledge. Using a functional approach, they identified five motives: values (humanitarian concern), understanding (learning about the illness), personal development, community concern and esteem enhancement. Interestingly, the more self-oriented motives (understanding, personal development, esteem) predicted who stayed longer better than purely other-oriented values. Example: Riya joins an NGO that supports cancer patients because she wants to learn about the illness and feel useful; if she is satisfied and feels part of the team, the model predicts she will still be volunteering a year later. Option b is right because Omoto and Snyder are the authors of this three-stage model of sustained volunteering. Exam trap: the distractors pair real researchers who work on other prosocial or cooperation topics, so the names look familiar from the same chapter, but none of them proposed a model of volunteering.", "traps": {"a": "C. Daniel Batson (1991) is known for the empathy-altruism hypothesis (helping out of empathic concern); there is no 'Batson and Gray' volunteer process model.", "c": "Marco Schmidt and Michael Tomasello (2012) study how young children understand and enforce social norms; their work is developmental, not a model of sustained volunteering.", "d": "Peter Richerson and Robert Boyd are cultural evolution theorists (Not by Genes Alone, 2005) who explain human cooperation through the spread of cultural norms, not the stages of volunteering."}}, {"q": "_____ is the form of prosocial behaviour, in which individual tries to reduce guilt and compensate for the harm although the effect isn't always direct at the person who was harmed.", "concept": "The answer is b: reparative altruism is prosocial behaviour aimed at reducing guilt and making up for harm, even when the help does not reach the person who was harmed. After we hurt someone, guilt is unpleasant, and helping repairs our sense of being a decent person. The striking point is that the repair need not be directed at the victim. J. Merrill Carlsmith and Alan Gross (1969) led participants to believe they had given painful electric shocks to a fellow participant (in fact a confederate) in a learning task. Later, when asked to make telephone calls for a campaign to save redwood trees, these 'guilty' participants agreed far more often than those who had only sounded a harmless buzzer, and compliance was highest when the request came not from the supposed victim but from someone who had watched them give the shocks. Example: Arjun snaps rudely at his younger brother before leaving home; on the way to college he gives the auto driver an unusually large tip and helps a stranger carry her bags. Option b is right because the stem lists the defining features: guilt reduction, compensation for harm, and help that is not always aimed at the person harmed. Exam trap: the image-repair idea also links wrongdoing to later helping, so examiners place it beside reparative altruism; but image repair is about restoring how others see you, while reparative altruism is about easing your own guilt. Key note: guilt-driven helping is sometimes discussed under the image-repair hypothesis, but the stem's wording (reduce guilt, compensate, not always aimed at the victim) is the definition of reparative altruism, so the answer used here, b, stands.", "traps": {"a": "The warm glow of success is Alice Isen's (1970) finding that people who have just succeeded on a task help more; it is helping from a good mood, not from guilt.", "c": "The image-repair hypothesis says that after a failure or wrongdoing people help to restore their damaged image in others' eyes, so it predicts helping mainly when others know of the failure or harm; the stem defines guilt-based compensation instead.", "d": "'Altruism hypothesis' is a loose name; the known theory is C. Daniel Batson's (1991) empathy-altruism hypothesis, in which helping springs from empathic concern for a person in need, not from guilt about harm one has caused."}}, {"q": "\"Aggressive energy\" held in check by inhibiting forces must be eventually discharged. Freud referred to such discharge as:", "concept": "The answer is d: Sigmund Freud called the discharge of pent-up aggressive energy catharsis. Sigmund Freud (1920), after the slaughter of the First World War, added a death instinct (thanatos) to his theory and held what is often called a hydraulic view of aggression: aggressive energy builds up inside, like water behind a dam, and if inhibiting forces hold it in check it must eventually be released, either in harmful acts or in safer outlets such as sport, shouting or fantasy. That release is catharsis, a Greek word for cleansing that Aristotle used for the emotional purging an audience feels at a tragedy, and that Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud (1895) used for the 'cathartic method' of talking out buried feelings. Konrad Lorenz (1966) later proposed a similar hydraulic model. Research does not support venting. Brad Bushman (2002) angered students with harsh comments on their essays, then had some hit a punching bag while thinking about the critic; afterwards they were more aggressive, not less, than students who had sat quietly. Example: Kabir is told to 'let it out' by punching pillows after a quarrel with a friend, and he ends up angrier. Option d is right because catharsis is Freud's term for discharging held-back aggressive energy. Exam trap: displacement also gives aggression an outlet, so it is the near miss; but displacement is about the target changing (the boss's anger lands on the dog), while catharsis is about the stored energy being released and supposedly used up.", "traps": {"a": "Displacement is redirecting aggression from its real source to a safer target (snapping at a younger sibling after being scolded at work); it concerns the target, not the discharge of energy as such.", "b": "Frustration is the blocking of a goal; in the frustration-aggression hypothesis of John Dollard and colleagues (1939) it is a cause of aggression, not its discharge.", "c": "Reactive (hostile) aggression is angry, impulsive aggression in response to provocation; it is a type of aggression, not Freud's name for releasing stored energy."}}, {"q": "Constructive conflict resolution occurs:", "concept": "The answer is c: constructive conflict resolution occurs when two parties cooperate and find a mutually acceptable alternative to their incompatible goals. Morton Deutsch (1973), in The Resolution of Conflict, separated two kinds of conflict process. In destructive conflict the parties compete, communication breaks down, suspicion grows and the dispute escalates until one side wins and the other loses, or both lose. In constructive conflict the parties treat the dispute as a shared problem, communicate openly and look for a solution that satisfies both, so the relationship survives and often improves. The best result is an integrative agreement (Dean Pruitt, 1981), which 'expands the pie' by trading on issues that each side values differently instead of simply splitting the difference. Example: Meera wants the flat quiet for studying in the evenings and her flatmate Priya wants to practise the guitar; instead of fighting, they agree that Priya will practise in the mornings while Meera is at the library, so both get what they really need. Option c is right because cooperation that produces a mutually acceptable alternative is the definition of a constructive outcome. Exam trap: option a sounds positive because it mentions agreement, but it describes bargaining in a mixed-motive situation, the process of negotiating, not the constructive result; option b, a compromise over a fixed pie, still leaves each side with only part of what it wanted.", "traps": {"a": "This describes bargaining or negotiation (offers and counter-offers aimed at agreement) in a mixed-motive situation where interests partly overlap; it is the process by which conflict may be settled, not the constructive outcome itself.", "b": "Mutual concessions to carve up a fixed-size pie is compromise, or distributive bargaining; each side gives up part of its goal, unlike the integrative, mutually satisfying solution of constructive resolution.", "d": "One party gaining while the other loses is a zero-sum, win-lose outcome, the mark of competitive, destructive conflict in Morton Deutsch's scheme."}}, {"q": "Fiedler proposed _____ of leadership based on ideas that there are two basic styles of leadership.", "concept": "The answer is b: Fred Fiedler proposed the contingency theory (contingency model) of leadership, built on the idea that there are two basic leadership styles. Fred Fiedler (1967) argued that no single style is best; effectiveness is contingent on the fit between the leader's style and the situation. Style is measured with the Least Preferred Co-worker (LPC) scale, on which the leader rates the person they have worked with least well. A leader who still describes that person fairly kindly (high LPC) is relationship-oriented; one who rates them harshly (low LPC) is task-oriented. Situational control, or favourableness, depends on three factors: leader-member relations (the most important), task structure, and position power (formal authority to reward and punish). Task-oriented leaders do best when control is very high or very low; relationship-oriented leaders do best when it is moderate. Because Fiedler saw style as a fairly fixed trait, he advised changing the situation to suit the leader rather than retraining the leader. Example: Arjun, a task-oriented captain, does well both with a chaotic new team that needs firm direction and with a loyal team on a clear-cut task, but his relationship-oriented friend Kavya does better with a fairly cooperative team facing a vague problem. Option b is right because 'contingent theory' is the stem's version of Fiedler's contingency model. Exam trap: the other options are real terms from the same syllabus, taken from power, learning and aggression, so they feel familiar, but none is a leadership theory built on two styles.", "traps": {"a": "Expert power is one of the bases of social power named by John French and Bertram Raven (1959): influence that comes from superior knowledge; it is a source of influence, not a leadership theory.", "c": "Social learning theory is Albert Bandura's (1977) account of learning by observing and imitating models; it explains how behaviour such as aggression is acquired, not leadership styles.", "d": "The cognitive neo-associationist model is Leonard Berkowitz's (1989) account of aggression, in which unpleasant events produce negative feelings that prime aggressive thoughts and actions."}}, {"q": "Whether or not disadvantaged, people try to change what is perceived as unfair social structure depends on:", "concept": "The answer is c: whether members of a disadvantaged group try to change an unfair social structure depends on the permeability and stability of that structure. Henri Tajfel and John Turner (1979), in social identity theory, argued that people seek a positive social identity from the groups they belong to. When their group compares badly with others, two features of the status system decide how they respond. Permeability is whether group boundaries can be crossed: if an individual can move up into the higher-status group, people choose social mobility, leaving the group as individuals instead of challenging the system. Stability is whether the status order looks fixed or open to change, and it works together with legitimacy (whether the order looks fair): if boundaries are closed and the hierarchy looks unstable and illegitimate, so that a different order can be imagined, people turn to social competition, collectively challenging the dominant group. If the hierarchy looks stable and legitimate, they fall back on social creativity, comparing on a new dimension ('we are poorer but kinder'). Example: Kabir studies at a low-ranked college. If good marks let him transfer to a top college (permeable boundaries), he works for his own transfer; if transfers are closed but the ranking system looks unfair and open to challenge (unstable), his classmates organise together to demand recognition. Option c is right because permeability and stability are exactly the two structural variables the theory names. Exam trap: the distractors borrow dimensions from stereotype research, so they sound scholarly; warmth (sociability) and competence are the two dimensions of the stereotype content model, and morality is a dimension on which groups are judged, not a property of the social structure.", "traps": {"a": "Stability is right, but sociability is not a structural variable: it is part of the warmth dimension in the stereotype content model of Susan Fiske and colleagues (2002), describing how friendly a group seems.", "b": "Permeability is right, but morality is not: it is a dimension on which groups are evaluated (Colin Leach, Naomi Ellemers and Manuela Barreto, 2007), not a feature of the status structure.", "d": "Competence and morality are both dimensions of how groups are perceived and evaluated (competence from the stereotype content model, morality from research on group virtue); neither decides between individual mobility and collective change."}}, {"q": "One way to reverse prejudice as shown in the Robbers Cave experiment is to:", "concept": "The answer is a: the Robbers Cave experiment showed that prejudice between groups can be reversed by fostering shared, or superordinate, goals. Muzafer Sherif (1954) and colleagues took 22 unacquainted boys aged about 11 to 12 to a summer camp at Robbers Cave State Park in Oklahoma. In phase 1, two groups lived apart, bonded and named themselves the Eagles and the Rattlers. In phase 2, a tournament with prizes (tug of war, baseball) produced name-calling, a burned flag and cabin raids: competition for scarce rewards created hostility, as realistic conflict theory predicts. In phase 3, the researchers first tried simple contact, such as shared meals and films, and it failed; meals turned into food fights. Hostility fell only when the staff arranged superordinate goals, which both groups wanted but neither could reach alone: restoring the camp's water supply, pooling money to rent a film and pulling a stalled food truck together. Friendships across the two groups rose afterwards. The full report appeared in 1961. Example: two rival hostel wings at Riya's college stop sniping at each other once they must jointly run the annual fest. Option a is right because shared goals that require cooperation are what reversed the prejudice. Exam trap: option d is tempting because contact can reduce prejudice, but at Robbers Cave mere contact during conflict made things worse; Gordon Allport's (1954) contact hypothesis says contact works only with equal status, common goals, cooperation and support from authorities.", "traps": {"b": "Educating the groups (giving them information about the other side) was not what ended the hostility at Robbers Cave; Muzafer Sherif's boys changed only when they had to cooperate for goals they all wanted.", "c": "Encouraging competition is what created the prejudice in phase 2; the tournament with prizes produced hostility, in line with realistic conflict theory.", "d": "Maintaining intergroup contact failed on its own: shared meals and films turned into food fights; contact helps only under Gordon Allport's (1954) conditions, which include a common goal."}}, {"q": "Comorbidity explains the notion that:", "concept": "The answer is d: comorbidity is the notion that differently defined disorders can occur together in the same person. In psychiatry, comorbidity means the presence of two or more distinct disorders at the same time, such as major depressive disorder with generalised anxiety disorder, or alcohol use disorder with post-traumatic stress disorder. In mental health it is the rule rather than the exception. The National Comorbidity Survey of Ronald Kessler and colleagues (1994) in the United States found that more than half of all lifetime disorders occurred in the roughly one person in seven who had a history of three or more disorders. Comorbidity matters for three reasons. It complicates diagnosis, because symptoms overlap. It worsens prognosis, since comorbid cases are usually more severe and harder to treat. And it raises questions about classification: if anxiety and depression so often travel together, perhaps they share causes, which is one reason for transdiagnostic treatments. Example: Meera is diagnosed with panic disorder and, a year later, also meets the criteria for major depression; her clinician has to plan treatment for both, not just one. Option d is right because the co-occurrence of separately defined disorders in one individual is exactly what comorbidity means. Exam trap: 'morbidity' sounds like 'mortality', so examiners offer options about dying or death; comorbidity is about illnesses co-existing in a living person, and it is also different from prevalence, which is how common a disorder is in a population.", "traps": {"a": "People with the same problem dying at the same time confuses comorbidity with mortality (death rates); comorbidity is about disorders co-existing in one living person.", "b": "A chronic fear of death is thanatophobia (death anxiety), a specific fear, not the co-occurrence of disorders.", "c": "Depression in winter refers to the seasonal pattern specifier of major depression (seasonal affective disorder), and even that is not 'always' in winter; it says nothing about two disorders occurring together."}}, {"q": "Which of the below given was historical explanation of psychopathology?", "concept": "The answer is a: witchcraft was a historical explanation of psychopathology. David Barlow and Mark Durand, in their textbook Abnormal Psychology: An Integrative Approach, describe three traditions that have competed to explain abnormal behaviour. The supernatural tradition blamed outside agents: demons, evil spirits, witches and even the moon and stars (the root of the word 'lunatic', from the Latin luna, moon). In late medieval Europe, strange behaviour was often put down to possession or witchcraft, and the witch-hunters' manual Malleus Maleficarum (1486) by Heinrich Kramer guided trials; treatments included exorcism, prayer and sometimes torture or execution. The biological tradition began with the Greek physician Hippocrates (born about 460 BC), who traced disorders to the brain and to an imbalance of the four humours. The psychological tradition includes moral therapy, psychoanalysis and behaviourism. Example: in a story told by Riya's grandmother, a young bride's sudden fits were blamed on a neighbour's black magic and an exorcist was called instead of a doctor; that is the supernatural explanation at work. Option a is right because witchcraft is a cause that people actually put forward to explain disordered behaviour. Exam trap: general paresis is the famous item from the history of psychiatry, so it feels like a historical explanation, but it is a disorder (syphilis of the brain), not an explanation; finding its cause gave strong support to the biological tradition, as when Richard von Krafft-Ebing (1897) reported that paretic patients inoculated with syphilitic material did not catch syphilis, so they must already have been infected. Key note: general paresis is part of the history of explanation, as the discovery that revived the biological tradition in the nineteenth century, but it is a disorder rather than an explanation, so the answer used here, a, stands.", "traps": {"b": "General paresis is a disorder (late-stage syphilis of the brain, with grandiosity, dementia and paralysis), not an explanation; discovering its bacterial cause supported the biological tradition.", "c": "Plague (the Black Death of the Middle Ages) was a deadly infectious disease and a source of terror, not a theory of why people develop abnormal behaviour.", "d": "Social class is a modern sociocultural risk factor (August Hollingshead and Fredrick Redlich, 1958, found more severe disorder in the lowest class), not one of the historical explanations."}}, {"q": "APA first published a predecessor of DSM as a statistical classification of institutionalized mental patients in:", "concept": "The answer is c: 1844 is the year usually given for the first predecessor of the DSM, a statistical classification of institutionalised mental patients credited to the forerunner of the American Psychiatric Association. The APA began that year as the Association of Medical Superintendents of American Institutions for the Insane, a body of asylum heads; it became the American Medico-Psychological Association in 1892 and took its present name in 1921. Many summaries of DSM history credit this new body with a statistical classification of the patients in its asylums in that founding year, which is why 1844 is given as the date of the first predecessor of the DSM. The push for classification in the United States was statistical. The 1840 census used a single category, 'idiocy/insanity', and the 1880 census separated seven: mania, melancholia, monomania, paresis, dementia, dipsomania and epilepsy. In 1918 the association, with the National Committee for Mental Hygiene, issued the Statistical Manual for the Use of Institutions for the Insane, the direct ancestor of the DSM. The first Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, DSM-I, followed in 1952, shaped partly by the army's classification from the Second World War. DSM-II came in 1968 and DSM-III, with explicit criteria, in 1980. Option c is right because 1844 is the date given for that first statistical classification, the earliest predecessor of the DSM. Exam trap: 1952 is the most familiar date in DSM history, so students pick it, but 1952 is DSM-I itself, not its predecessor; 1905 is the year of the Binet-Simon intelligence scale, and 1852 is a filler. Key note: 1844 is firmly the founding year of the association, but the APA's own history of the DSM begins with the 1840 and 1880 censuses and does not describe an 1844 classification; the claim comes from widely repeated summaries. Of the options, 1844 is the only date tied to the APA's origins, so the answer used here, c, stands.", "traps": {"a": "1852 is a filler date with no landmark in the DSM's history; the census landmarks are 1840 (a single 'idiocy/insanity' category) and 1880 (seven categories).", "b": "1905 is the year Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon published their intelligence scale, a landmark in testing, not in psychiatric classification.", "d": "1952 is the year DSM-I itself was published, more than a century after the 1844 predecessor the stem asks about."}}, {"q": "Psychopathology is unlikely to result from the impact of any single factor. This is one of the features of:", "concept": "The answer is b: the idea that psychopathology is unlikely to result from any single factor is a feature of the diathesis-stress model. A diathesis is a vulnerability, usually inherited but sometimes acquired (early trauma, a negative thinking style), that makes a person more likely to develop a disorder. The disorder appears only when a stressor, such as a loss, an illness or a major life change, acts on that vulnerability. Neither alone is usually enough: many people with the vulnerability never become ill because they never meet enough stress, and many people under severe stress stay well because they carry little vulnerability. The greater the vulnerability, the less stress it takes. Avshalom Caspi and colleagues (2003) gave a well-known example: people with a short version of the serotonin transporter gene were more likely to become depressed, but mainly if they had faced several stressful life events or childhood maltreatment. Example: Meera and her cousin both lose their jobs in the same month; Meera, whose mother and grandmother had major depression, becomes depressed, while her cousin, with no such vulnerability, recovers within weeks. Option b is right because the model rests on the claim that vulnerability and stress must combine. Exam trap: the reciprocal gene-environment model and mind-body interaction also belong to the modern integrative view, so a 'both' option looks safe; but they describe how genes shape environments and how mind and body affect each other, not the classic vulnerability-plus-trigger formula. Key note: the reciprocal gene-environment model also rejects a single cause, but the classic model built on the idea that no single factor is enough is the diathesis-stress model, so the answer used here, b, stands.", "traps": {"a": "The reciprocal gene-environment model says genes raise the chance of meeting certain environments (an impulsive temperament invites more stressful life events), which then trigger disorder; it explains how genes shape stress rather than stating the vulnerability-plus-stress formula.", "c": "Mind and body interaction is the general principle that psychological states change biology and the reverse (stress weakening immunity, therapy changing brain activity); it is not the model the stem describes.", "d": "Both (A) and (C) fails because neither named idea is the model built on vulnerability plus stress; only b fits."}}, {"q": "An adolescent dealing with unacceptable social inadequacy attempts to mask those feeling by seeking oral gratification. This is an example of:", "concept": "The answer is d: seeking oral gratification to cope with painful feelings of social inadequacy is an example of regression. Sigmund Freud (1894) first described defences as unconscious strategies the ego uses to protect itself from anxiety, and his daughter Anna Freud (1936) catalogued them in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence. Regression is retreating, under stress, to behaviour typical of an earlier, safer stage of development. Freud's first psychosexual stage is the oral stage (roughly the first year to eighteen months), in which pleasure centres on the mouth through sucking and feeding. So oral habits such as overeating, chewing pens, nail-biting, smoking or thumb-sucking are the classic signs of regression in an older person. The adolescent in the stem cannot face the feeling of being socially inadequate, so instead of dealing with it at an adult level he falls back on the comfort the mouth gave him as an infant. Other everyday examples are a six-year-old who starts wetting the bed again after a sibling is born, or an adult who sulks and throws a tantrum when criticised. Option d is right because returning to oral comforts under stress is the textbook pattern of regression. Exam trap: the word 'mask' pulls students towards reaction formation, which does hide a feeling; but reaction formation hides it by acting out the opposite (loud bravado covering inadequacy), not by returning to an earlier stage's comforts. Fixation is the related idea that part of the libido stays stuck at one stage.", "traps": {"a": "Repression is pushing threatening thoughts, memories or wishes out of consciousness; the adolescent is not forgetting the feeling but falling back on infantile comforts.", "b": "Reaction formation is behaving in the opposite way to an unacceptable feeling (hiding inadequacy behind loud bravado); oral gratification is not the opposite of feeling inadequate.", "c": "Rationalisation is giving plausible but false reasons for one's behaviour ('I skip parties because they waste time'); the stem describes a retreat in behaviour, not an excuse."}}, {"q": "Neurotransmitters that are studied in psychopathology include _____ which can produce states of high arousal and _____ which inhibit nerve impulse.", "concept": "The answer is a: norepinephrine can produce states of high arousal, and GABA inhibits nerve impulses. Norepinephrine (noradrenaline) is a catecholamine made mainly by neurons of the locus coeruleus, a small nucleus in the pons whose axons reach almost the whole brain. It raises alertness, vigilance and arousal, and in the body it is the sympathetic nervous system's transmitter for the fight-or-flight response. Overactivity of this system is linked to panic attacks and to the hyperarousal of anxiety and post-traumatic stress disorder. GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid) is the main inhibitory neurotransmitter of the brain. When it binds to GABA-A receptors, chloride ions flow into the neuron and make it less likely to fire. Low GABA activity is linked to anxiety, and benzodiazepines (such as diazepam) and alcohol calm people because they strengthen GABA's effect. Example: when Kabir hears a loud crash at night, his locus coeruleus fires and noradrenaline makes him instantly alert, heart pounding; a sedative such as diazepam would damp this down by boosting GABA. Option a is right because it puts the arousing transmitter in the first blank and the inhibitory one in the second, exactly as the stem requires. Exam trap: the options reuse the same few transmitters in different orders, testing whether you know which one inhibits; GABA (and glycine in the spinal cord) inhibits, glutamate is the main excitatory transmitter, and none of norepinephrine, epinephrine, dopamine or serotonin is the brain's classic inhibitor.", "traps": {"b": "This reverses the roles: GABA calms rather than arouses, and dopamine is mainly linked to reward, movement and psychosis, not to inhibiting nerve impulses.", "c": "Epinephrine (adrenaline) does produce arousal, but mainly as a hormone from the adrenal medulla; dopamine is not the classic inhibitory transmitter, so the second blank fails.", "d": "Serotonin regulates mood, sleep, appetite and impulse control rather than producing high arousal, and norepinephrine excites rather than inhibits, so both blanks are wrong."}}, {"q": "The relational self is a concept from social psychology that incorporates ideas from:", "concept": "The answer is b: the relational self incorporates ideas from object relations and attachment theories. Susan Andersen and Serena Chen (2002), in Psychological Review, proposed that the self is not one fixed structure but is partly made of 'self-with-other' knowledge. For each significant other (a parent, a sibling, a partner) we store a mental representation of that person, linked to a version of ourselves as we are with them. When we meet someone new who resembles a significant other, that representation is activated automatically, and we start to feel, expect and even act like the self we are with that person. Andersen calls this social-cognitive transference, a laboratory version of Freud's transference. The concept draws on two older traditions. Object relations theory (Melanie Klein, Ronald Fairbairn, Donald Winnicott) holds that we internalise representations of self and others from early relationships. John Bowlby's (1969) attachment theory holds that early care builds internal working models of the self and of others that guide later relationships. Example: Arjun's new manager has the same stern, quiet manner as his father; without realising it, Arjun becomes the anxious, eager-to-please son he was at home. Option b is right because these two theories supply the internalised self-other representations at the heart of the concept. Exam trap: exchange and communal relationships sound relational, but they are relationship types from Margaret Clark and Judson Mills (1979), not source theories of the relational self. Key note: Susan Andersen has written that her approach draws directly on Harry Stack Sullivan's (1953) interpersonal theory, so a has real truth, but the standard description names object relations and attachment theories, so the answer used here, b, stands.", "traps": {"a": "Interpersonal theories, above all Harry Stack Sullivan's (1953), hold that personality exists in relationships and are a real influence on Andersen's work, but the concept is usually described as drawing on object relations and attachment theories.", "c": "Exchange relationships are one of Margaret Clark and Judson Mills's (1979) two relationship types, in which partners give benefits expecting a comparable return; it is a kind of relationship, not a theory behind the relational self.", "d": "Communal relationships are Clark and Mills's (1979) other type, in which partners give in response to each other's needs without keeping score; again a relationship type, not a source theory."}}, {"q": "In the Stroop tasks, interference is measured by:", "concept": "The answer is a: in the Stroop task, interference is measured by how long it takes to name the colour of the ink in a list of words. John Ridley Stroop (1935) printed colour words in conflicting ink colours, so that the word RED appeared in green ink. When people simply read the words aloud, the clashing colours barely slowed them. But when they had to name the ink colour and ignore the word, they took about 74 percent longer than when naming the colours of plain patches. That extra time is the Stroop interference effect. The explanation is automaticity: for a literate adult, reading is so practised that a word's meaning comes to mind involuntarily and competes with the weaker, less practised colour-naming response, which then has to win against it. Naming the ink is therefore the slow, effortful task, and the time difference between conflicting words and neutral colour patches is the measure. The task is now widely used to test selective attention and executive control, and an 'emotional Stroop' version shows that anxious people are slower to name the ink of threat-related words. Example: Riya sees BLUE printed in yellow ink and must say 'yellow'; she hesitates because 'blue' jumps into her mind first. Option a is right because the slowing of colour naming is exactly what interference refers to. Exam trap: option b, reading the word list, is the task that shows almost no interference, because word reading is automatic; examiners swap the two tasks.", "traps": {"b": "Reading the list of words is the fast, automatic task; John Ridley Stroop found that conflicting ink colours barely slowed reading, so it shows little interference.", "c": "Checking distortion in people is not part of the Stroop task; it is a filler with no link to the conflict between word and ink colour.", "d": "Changing a feeling is also unrelated filler; the Stroop task times colour naming, not emotional change, even though the emotional Stroop uses emotional words."}}, {"q": "Which among the given options is a predominant evolutionary theory of phobias?", "concept": "The answer is c: biological preparedness is the leading evolutionary theory of phobias. Martin Seligman (1971) argued that evolution has prepared humans to learn fears of things that threatened our ancestors, such as snakes, spiders, heights, deep water and angry faces, far more easily than fears of modern dangers such as electric sockets or cars. Prepared fears can form after a single bad experience, or even from watching someone else, and they resist extinction. This explains why phobias cluster around a short list of ancestral objects. Strong evidence comes from rhesus monkeys: Michael Cook and Susan Mineka (1989) showed laboratory-reared monkeys videos of other monkeys reacting with fear; they learned to fear toy snakes and toy crocodiles, but not flowers or toy rabbits edited into the same footage. Arne Ohman and Susan Mineka (2001) later described an evolved \"fear module\" in the brain that such cues trigger selectively. Example: Riya has never been bitten, yet one scream from her cousin at a garden snake leaves her with a lasting snake phobia, while a far more dangerous shock from a faulty switch leaves no phobia at all. Option c is right because preparedness is the evolutionary explanation that dominates textbook accounts of phobias. Exam trap: a learned fear response (option b) sounds scientific but is plain conditioning with no evolutionary claim, while the non-associative account (option a) is also evolutionary, so students who spot only that word may pick it. Key note: Ross Menzies and John Clarke (1995) proposed non-associative fear acquisition (reviewed by Richie Poulton and Ross Menzies, 2002), in which some fears (of heights, water, strangers) are innate and appear without any learning; it is evolutionary too, but a minority view, and preparedness remains the predominant evolutionary account, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Non-associative fear acquisition is the view of Richie Poulton and Ross Menzies (2002) that some fears are innate and need no learning at all. It is evolutionary, but a minority position, so it is not the predominant theory the stem asks for.", "b": "A learned fear response is classical conditioning, as when John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner (1920) conditioned Little Albert to fear a white rat. It is a learning explanation and makes no claim about evolution.", "d": "Specific phobia acquisition is not the name of any theory; specific phobia is a DSM-5 diagnosis. Stanley Rachman's (1977) three pathways (conditioning, vicarious learning, information) explain how phobias are acquired, but they are not an evolutionary theory either."}}, {"q": "Individuals with somatoform disorders may often display a surprising indifference about the symptoms especially when the symptoms to most people would be disturbing. This is known as:", "concept": "The answer is c: la belle indifférence, French for \"beautiful indifference\", is the calm, almost cheerful unconcern some patients show about a dramatic physical symptom. It is classically described in conversion disorder, which DSM-IV placed among the somatoform disorders and DSM-5 calls functional neurological symptom disorder, now in the group of somatic symptom and related disorders. In conversion disorder a person loses a motor or sensory function (a paralysed arm, sudden blindness, loss of speech, seizures) with no neurological disease that explains it. The phrase comes from nineteenth-century work on hysteria: Sigmund Freud (1895) used it in Studies on Hysteria for his patient Elisabeth von R. and later credited it to his Paris teacher, the neurologist Jean-Martin Charcot. It is often wrongly credited to Pierre Janet, who wrote about such indifference but does not seem to have used the phrase. Example: Meera wakes up unable to move her right arm the morning after a bitter family quarrel. Doctors find normal reflexes and nerve tests, yet she smiles and says it is not really bothering her. Most people would be terrified; her calm is la belle indifférence. Option c is right because it is the standard clinical term for this surprising lack of concern. Exam trap: the sign is less useful than its fame suggests. DSM-5 calls it non-specific, and a systematic review by Jon Stone and colleagues (2006) found it in a median of 21 percent of conversion patients but 29 percent of patients with real organic disease, so it must never be used to rule out a neurological illness. The other three options are distortions of French or Italian phrases built to look like the real term.", "traps": {"a": "Viva la difference garbles the French saying \"vive la différence\" (\"long live the difference\"), a light-hearted celebration of the differences between the sexes. It is not a clinical term.", "b": "Quella difference is a made-up blend of the Italian word \"quella\" (\"that\") and the English word difference; it names nothing in psychopathology.", "d": "Quesce quasa la difference is nonsense dressed up to look French. Only la belle indifférence describes the patient's calm unconcern about a frightening symptom."}}, {"q": "In schizophrenia when an individual has disorganised speech, the term 'clanging' refers to:", "concept": "The answer is a: clanging means choosing words for their sound, especially rhyme, rather than their meaning. Disorganised speech is one of the five DSM-5 criterion A symptoms of schizophrenia, and it reflects formal thought disorder, a breakdown in the links between ideas that we infer from how a person talks. In clanging (clang associations) the next word is pulled in by how the last one sounded, so speech follows rhymes and puns instead of a line of thought. Example: asked how he slept, Arjun says, \"I slept, wept, kept the cat, the hat, the bat is flat.\" Each word echoes the sound of the one before, but the sentence goes nowhere. Clanging is also common in mania, where it appears inside flight of ideas, so it is not unique to schizophrenia. Option a is right because it is the only option built on sound, the defining feature of clanging; the word \"only\" overstates it, since clanging speech still mixes in ordinary words, but rhyme is the marker. Exam trap: all four options describe real kinds of disorganised speech, so the item tests whether you can match each label to its description. Irrelevant answers are tangentiality, unfinished sentences are thought blocking or derailment, and speech that is neither structured nor understandable is incoherence (word salad). Only clanging is defined by rhyme.", "traps": {"b": "Answers that are not relevant to the question describe tangentiality, where the person drifts away from the point and never reaches the goal of the reply. It concerns relevance, not sound.", "c": "Stopping before a sentence is complete describes thought blocking (a sudden halt, the mind going blank) or derailment, where the speaker slips off track mid-sentence. Neither involves rhyme.", "d": "Speech with no structure that cannot be understood is incoherence, also called word salad, the most severe form of disorganised speech. Clanging speech can be partly followed; its oddity is the sound links between words."}}, {"q": "Cognitive therapy, when added to exposure for PTSD, is particularly helpful in addressing:", "concept": "The answer is d: when cognitive therapy is added to exposure for post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD), its special contribution is reducing trauma-related guilt (and the shame that often goes with it). Exposure therapies, such as the prolonged exposure of Edna Foa and Barbara Rothbaum (1998), have the person repeatedly relive the trauma memory and face safe reminders, so fear habituates and avoidance drops. But fear is not the only emotion in PTSD. Many survivors blame themselves (\"I should have stopped it\", \"I froze, so it is my fault\"), and these appraisals do not always fade with exposure alone, because guilt rests on beliefs about responsibility rather than on a learned fear. Cognitive therapy targets those beliefs directly: the therapist helps the person examine hindsight bias (judging past choices with knowledge that came only later) and weigh what was really in their control. Cognitive processing therapy (Patricia Resick and Monica Schnicke, 1992) works on such \"stuck points\", and a 2002 trial by Resick's group with rape survivors found it matched prolonged exposure on PTSD symptoms and did better on two of four guilt subscales. Example: Kabir survived a road accident in which his friend died. After exposure sessions he can drive past the spot without panic, but he still thinks, \"I chose that road, so I killed him.\" Cognitive work on this belief eases his guilt. Option d is right because guilt is the problem on which cognitive techniques add most to exposure. Exam trap: suicidal risk and relapse sound like weighty clinical targets, so they tempt, but they are general concerns in every treatment, not the specific gain from adding cognitive therapy.", "traps": {"a": "Suicidal tendencies are assessed and managed in any PTSD treatment through risk assessment and safety planning. Cognitive therapy is not singled out as the add-on that targets them.", "b": "Lowering the risk of relapse is a general aim of all psychological treatment, not the particular problem that adding cognitive work to exposure solves in PTSD.", "c": "Depersonalisation, feeling detached from one's own body or self, is a dissociative symptom (seen in depersonalisation-derealisation disorder and the dissociative subtype of PTSD). It is not the classic target that cognitive therapy adds to exposure."}}, {"q": "Which of the following psychotherapies have obtained support in the treatment of MDD?", "concept": "The answer is d: cognitive therapy has the largest body of research support among the listed psychotherapies for major depressive disorder (MDD). Aaron Beck, John Rush, Brian Shaw and Gary Emery (1979) built it on the idea that depression is kept going by negative automatic thoughts about the self, the world and the future (the cognitive triad). Therapist and client treat these thoughts as hypotheses, test them against evidence and through behavioural experiments, and replace distortions such as overgeneralisation. Many trials show it works about as well as antidepressants for most patients and protects better against relapse after treatment ends. Two other options are also evidence-based. Interpersonal psychotherapy, from Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman (1984), works on grief, role disputes, role transitions and interpersonal deficits; in the NIMH Treatment of Depression Collaborative Research Program (Irene Elkin and colleagues, 1989) it did about as well as cognitive behaviour therapy. Behavioural activation schedules rewarding activity: Neil Jacobson and colleagues (1996) found it as effective as full cognitive therapy, and Sona Dimidjian and colleagues (2006) found it matched medication in severe depression. Example: Arjun stops seeing friends after losing his job and thinks, \"I am useless.\" Cognitive therapy tests that thought; behavioural activation would get him out walking with friends again. Option d is right because cognitive therapy is the most researched psychological treatment for MDD. Exam trap: with three supported options and no \"all of these\", students freeze; pick the treatment with the largest evidence base, and never psychoanalytic therapy (option c), the only one with weak support. Key note: interpersonal psychotherapy and behavioural activation are also well supported, so three options are true and the item has no clean single answer; cognitive therapy is keyed as the most researched; d stands, and only c is plainly wrong.", "traps": {"a": "Interpersonal psychotherapy (Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman, 1984) is a well-supported treatment for depression that focuses on current relationships and role changes. It is not keyed only because cognitive therapy has the larger evidence base.", "b": "Behavioural activation, which rebuilds rewarding activity, has strong support (it matched medication in severe depression in the 2006 trial by Sona Dimidjian and colleagues). It is not keyed because its evidence base is smaller than that of cognitive therapy.", "c": "Psychoanalytic therapy, the long-term exploration of unconscious conflict in Sigmund Freud's tradition, has the weakest controlled evidence of the four for MDD; only shorter psychodynamic therapies have modest trial support. This is the one option that is plainly wrong."}}, {"q": "The most commonly used psychological treatment for obsessive-compulsive and related disorders is:", "concept": "The answer is d: exposure and response prevention (ERP) is the most widely used and best supported psychological treatment for obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD), and it is also used for related disorders. Victor Meyer (1966) first described it for obsessional rituals, and Edna Foa and colleagues refined and tested it from the 1980s onwards. It has two parts. In exposure, the person deliberately faces the thought, object or situation that triggers the obsession, usually step by step up a hierarchy. In response prevention, the person does not perform the compulsion that normally follows. The logic is behavioural: the ritual brings quick relief, so it is negatively reinforced, and it stops the person from learning that the anxiety would fade on its own and the feared disaster would not happen. Blocking it allows habituation and new learning. Studies by Foa's team that tested the two parts separately found that exposure alone mainly lowered anxiety, response prevention alone mainly cut rituals, and the combination worked best. Example: Meera fears germs and washes her hands 40 times a day. Her therapist has her touch a dustbin lid and then sit without washing; her anxiety peaks, then falls over the hour, and peaks lower in the next session. Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors and clomipramine are the main drug options, but ERP is the first-line psychological treatment. Option d is right because it names the full package that research supports. Exam trap: imaginal exposure and in-vivo exposure are real parts of ERP, so they look correct, but exposure without response prevention leaves the ritual free to neutralise the fear, and gains are much weaker.", "traps": {"a": "Coping strategies that prevent processing the problem describe avoidance and distraction, which keep OCD going because the person never learns that the fear is groundless. ERP does the opposite.", "b": "Imaginal exposure, facing the feared scene in imagination (for example, picturing the house burning down after leaving the stove unchecked), is a component of ERP, useful when the feared outcome cannot be staged. Without response prevention it is not the standard treatment.", "c": "In-vivo exposure, facing the real trigger, is the core of the exposure half of ERP. Without blocking the compulsion it is incomplete, because the ritual keeps relieving and so reinforcing the fear."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is helpful in GAD?", "concept": "The answer is a: a psychological treatment combining relaxation training with cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT) is the established helpful treatment for generalised anxiety disorder (GAD). In DSM-5, GAD is excessive, hard-to-control worry about many everyday matters on more days than not for at least six months, with symptoms such as restlessness, fatigue, poor concentration, irritability, muscle tension and disturbed sleep. Thomas Borkovec's avoidance theory of worry (Thomas Borkovec and colleagues, 2004) holds that worry is a verbal way of avoiding vivid, frightening images and the bodily arousal that goes with them; because worry dampens that arousal, it is negatively reinforced and continues. Treatment therefore has two arms. Relaxation (progressive muscle relaxation, or the applied relaxation of Lars-Goran Ost, 1987) brings down chronic tension and arousal. Cognitive therapy teaches the person to catch worry, test the predicted catastrophes, face worry images instead of avoiding them, and tolerate uncertainty. Example: Riya, a hostel student, lies awake worrying about exams, money and her parents' health. She learns to relax her shoulders on cue and to write down, test and challenge each \"what if\". Trials show such treatment works and, unlike drugs, its gains last after therapy ends. Option a is right because it is the psychological approach with lasting benefit in GAD. Exam trap: electroconvulsive therapy (option c) belongs to severe depression, and psychoanalytic therapy (option d) has little controlled evidence in GAD, so neither should tempt. Key note: pharmacotherapy (option b) is also helpful in GAD, so b is not false; relaxation plus CBT is keyed as the psychological treatment with lasting gains; a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Pharmacotherapy (selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors, serotonin and noradrenaline reuptake inhibitors, buspirone, and benzodiazepines only short term because of dependence) does reduce GAD symptoms, but relapse is common when the drug is stopped. It is the drug option, not the psychological treatment with lasting gains.", "c": "Electroconvulsive therapy induces a brief seizure under anaesthesia and is used for severe, psychotic or treatment-resistant depression and for catatonia. It has no role in GAD.", "d": "Psychoanalytic therapy, which looks for the unconscious conflict behind anxiety, has little controlled evidence in GAD and is not a recommended first treatment."}}, {"q": "Addition, subtraction, multiplication and division are not possible in _____ measurement scale.", "concept": "The answer is a: on a nominal scale the numbers are only labels, so addition, subtraction, multiplication and division mean nothing. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) sorted measurement into four levels, each adding one property. A nominal scale has identity only: numbers name categories with no order (jersey numbers, roll numbers, coding male as 1 and female as 2). You can only count how many cases fall in each category, find the mode and run chi-square. An ordinal scale adds order (class ranks, ratings), but the gaps between ranks are not equal, so the median and Spearman's rank correlation fit. An interval scale adds equal units but has no true zero (Celsius temperature, IQ); adding and subtracting are meaningful, but ratios are not, so 40 degrees is not twice as hot as 20. A ratio scale adds a true zero (weight, height, reaction time), so all four operations work and 60 kg really is twice 30 kg. Example: Arjun wears shirt number 10 and Kabir number 5. Adding them gives 15 and dividing gives 2, but neither result says anything about the two players. Option a is right because the nominal scale is the only level at which no arithmetic at all, not even ordering, is meaningful. Exam trap: interval tempts because ratios fail there, but addition and subtraction do work on interval data; the stem asks for the scale on which none of the four operations is possible. Key note: ordinal numbers do not support arithmetic either, but nominal is the scale where no arithmetic (not even ordering) is meaningful, the textbook answer; a stands.", "traps": {"b": "An ordinal scale ranks cases (first, second, third), so it supports order, the median and percentiles. Its intervals are unequal, so arithmetic on ranks is shaky too, but it carries more information than nominal labels and is not the textbook answer.", "c": "An interval scale has equal units without a true zero (Celsius, IQ); addition and subtraction are meaningful there, and only multiplication and division (ratios) are not.", "d": "A ratio scale has equal units and a true zero (weight, reaction time), so all four operations, including ratios such as twice as heavy, are meaningful."}}, {"q": "In which type of interview the topics and issues to be covered are specified in advance, however, the sequence and wording of the question in the course of the interview is decided by the interviewer?", "concept": "The answer is b: in the interview guide approach the topics and issues are listed in advance in outline form, but the interviewer decides the order and wording of questions as the interview unfolds. Michael Quinn Patton (1980), in Qualitative Evaluation Methods, described three approaches to qualitative interviewing and, in a summary table, set them beside the closed, fixed-response interview of quantitative work, running from least to most structured. In the informal conversational interview, questions arise naturally from the immediate context, with no set topics or wording. In the interview guide approach, a checklist of topics makes sure every respondent is asked about the same areas, while the interviewer keeps the conversation natural. In the standardised open-ended interview, the exact wording and sequence of every question are fixed in advance, but the answers are open. In the closed, fixed-response interview, both questions and answer categories are fixed, and the respondent picks from them. Each step along the line buys comparability and easier analysis at the price of flexibility and depth. The interview guide approach is close to what clinicians and survey researchers call a semi-structured interview. Example: Riya studies homesickness among first-year hostel students. Her guide lists family contact, food, friendships and sleep; with one student she starts with food, with another with friendships, phrasing each question in the student's own words. Option b is right because it is the only type with fixed topics but flexible sequence and wording. Exam trap: the standardised open-ended interview also uses open questions, so students confuse the two; the difference is that it fixes the exact wording and order, which this stem says the interviewer decides.", "traps": {"a": "The informal conversational interview has no topics set in advance; questions emerge spontaneously from the conversation. The stem says the topics are specified beforehand, which rules it out.", "c": "The standardised open-ended interview fixes the exact wording and sequence of every question before the interview, leaving only the answers open. The interviewer has no freedom over order or wording.", "d": "The closed, fixed-response interview fixes both the questions and the answer categories, like a spoken questionnaire. It is the most rigid of Michael Quinn Patton's four types, the opposite of the flexibility the stem describes."}}, {"q": "Which among the following is not the measure of central tendency?", "concept": "The answer is d: mean deviation is a measure of dispersion (variability), not of central tendency. A measure of central tendency gives one typical value that represents a whole set of scores. There are three main ones. The arithmetic mean is the sum of the scores divided by their number; it uses every score but is pulled by extreme values. The median is the middle score when the scores are put in order; it resists outliers and suits skewed or ordinal data. The mode is the most frequent score and the only one usable with nominal data. Mean deviation answers a different question: how far, on average, do the scores lie from the centre? It is the average of the absolute deviations of the scores from the mean (or median), with signs ignored. Example: Meera's three test scores are 2, 4 and 6. The mean is 4 and the median is 4. The deviations from the mean are 2, 0 and 2, so the mean deviation is 4 divided by 3, about 1.33 marks. The mean and median locate the centre; the mean deviation describes the spread around it. Option d is right because mean deviation describes scatter, not a typical value. Exam trap: mean deviation is the favourite decoy precisely because it contains the word \"mean\"; range, quartile deviation, standard deviation and variance are the other measures of spread that can appear in this slot.", "traps": {"a": "The arithmetic mean, the sum of scores divided by their number, is the most used measure of central tendency, so it cannot be the exception.", "b": "The mode, the most frequently occurring score, is a measure of central tendency and the only one that works with nominal categories.", "c": "The median, the middle score of an ordered set (the 50th percentile), is a measure of central tendency that resists extreme scores."}}, {"q": "The degree to which numerical data tend to spread about an average value is known as:", "concept": "The answer is b: dispersion is the general name for the degree to which scores spread out around an average value. It is also called variability or scatter. Two groups can share the same average and still differ sharply in how bunched or spread out their scores are, so a good description of data needs both a measure of central tendency and a measure of dispersion. Example: Riya's class and Arjun's class both have a mean of 60 marks in psychology. In Riya's class every student scored between 55 and 65; in Arjun's, scores ran from 20 to 95. The averages match, but dispersion is far greater in Arjun's class, and a teacher would plan very differently for each. Dispersion is measured in several ways. The range is the highest score minus the lowest. The quartile deviation is half the distance between the first and third quartiles. The mean deviation is the average absolute deviation from the mean. The variance is the average squared deviation from the mean, and the standard deviation is its square root, the most widely used measure. Option b is right because the stem defines the concept itself, while every other option is either one tool for measuring it or a different statistic altogether. Exam trap: standard deviation is so closely tied to spread that students pick it, but the stem asks for the name of the property, and standard deviation is only its best-known measure.", "traps": {"a": "The range, the highest score minus the lowest, is the crudest measure of dispersion because it rests on only two scores. It is one way to measure spread, not the name of the concept.", "c": "Standard deviation, the square root of the average squared deviation from the mean, is the most used measure of dispersion. The stem defines dispersion itself, not this particular index.", "d": "Standard error is the standard deviation of a sampling distribution, such as the spread of sample means around the population mean (SD divided by the square root of N). It describes the precision of a statistic, not the spread of raw scores around their average."}}, {"q": "Which of the following curves is known as a normal probability curve?", "concept": "The answer is b: the normal probability curve is mesokurtic. Kurtosis describes how peaked or flat a distribution is compared with the normal curve, and how heavy its tails are. Karl Pearson (1905) introduced the three terms, from the Greek kurtos, \"curved\" or \"bulging\". A mesokurtic curve (meso, \"middle\") has the moderate peakedness of the normal curve. A leptokurtic curve (lepto, \"thin\") is taller and narrower, with scores packed around the mean and heavier tails. A platykurtic curve (platy, \"flat\") is flatter and broader, with scores spread more evenly. On the moment measure the normal curve has a kurtosis of 3; software such as SPSS reports excess kurtosis (kurtosis minus 3), so a mesokurtic curve scores 0. Indian statistics texts also use the percentile coefficient, Ku = Q divided by (P90 minus P10), which is 0.263 for the normal curve; a larger value means platykurtic, a smaller one leptokurtic. The normal curve itself, derived by Abraham de Moivre (1733), is bell-shaped, symmetrical and unimodal, with mean, median and mode equal. Example: if most of Kabir's classmates score very close to the class average, with only a few far out, the curve is leptokurtic; if scores spread fairly evenly from low to high, it is platykurtic. Option b is right because mesokurtic means normal peakedness, the defining shape of the normal probability curve. Exam trap: \"Normo kurtic\" sounds like \"normal\" and is planted to catch guessers, but it is not a statistical term.", "traps": {"a": "A leptokurtic curve is more peaked than the normal curve, with scores crowded near the mean and heavier tails (kurtosis above 3). It departs from normality.", "c": "A platykurtic curve is flatter and broader than the normal curve (kurtosis below 3), as when scores spread fairly evenly across the range. It also departs from normality.", "d": "Normo kurtic is not a standard statistical term; it is a decoy built from the word normal. The kurtosis of the normal curve is called mesokurtic."}}, {"q": "The formula for converting raw score into Z score is:", "concept": "The answer is d: a z score is found by subtracting the mean from the raw score and dividing by the standard deviation, Z = (X − M)/σ. A z score (standard score) tells you how many standard deviations a raw score lies above or below the mean of its group. The subtraction X minus M gives the distance from the mean in raw units and keeps the sign: positive if the score is above the mean, negative if below. Dividing by σ turns that distance into standard deviation units, so scores from different tests become comparable. A full set of z scores always has a mean of 0 and a standard deviation of 1. Example: Riya scores 70 in a psychology test where the class mean is 60 and the SD is 5, so z = (70 minus 60) divided by 5 = 2; she is two SDs above the mean, ahead of about 98 percent of a normal group. Kabir scores 55, so his z is minus 1, one SD below the mean. If Riya also scores 80 in statistics, where the mean is 75 and the SD 10, her z there is only 0.5, so psychology is her relatively stronger subject even though the raw mark is lower. Option d is right because it is the only formula that measures distance from the mean in SD units with the correct sign. Exam trap: option a uses the same parts with the subtraction reversed; it gives the right size but the wrong sign, so a score above the mean would come out negative. Derived scores such as T = 50 + 10z are built on this formula.", "traps": {"a": "(M − X)/σ reverses the subtraction. It gives the right distance but the wrong sign, so a score above the mean would get a negative z.", "b": "(M − σ)/X subtracts the standard deviation from the mean and divides by the raw score; this has no statistical meaning and does not measure a score's distance from the mean.", "c": "(σ − M)/X is another meaningless arrangement of the same symbols, with the raw score in the denominator; no standard score is computed this way."}}, {"q": "Measurement error can be eliminated/reduced by:", "concept": "The answer is c: taking a large sample reduces error, because random errors tend to cancel out as more observations are added. Every observed score or sample statistic contains some error. Random error pushes a result up on one occasion and down on another, with no fixed direction, so when many observations are averaged the ups and downs offset each other. In sampling, this is why the standard error of the mean equals the SD divided by the square root of N: quadrupling the sample halves the error. In testing, the same logic explains why adding items or observations raises reliability (the Spearman-Brown formula of Charles Spearman and William Brown, 1910). Example: Meera wants the average reaction time of college students. If she tests 10 students, one unusually fast or slow person can shift her mean a lot; with 400 students such chance quirks average out, and her estimate sits much closer to the true value. What a big sample cannot fix is systematic error (bias). In 1936 the Literary Digest magazine collected more than 2 million straw-poll ballots and still predicted the wrong winner of the United States presidential election, because its mailing lists (telephone directories, car registrations, its own subscribers) and its self-selected repliers did not represent the voters. Option c is right because sample size is the only option that actually lowers error. Exam trap: standard error sounds like a remedy, but it estimates how large the error is; it does not reduce it. Key note: strictly, a large sample reduces sampling error, and random measurement error averages out but systematic error does not; c is still the only option that reduces error; c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Parametric tests (t-test, ANOVA, Pearson's r) are ways of analysing data that assume normality and interval scores. Choosing them does not change how much error the scores already contain.", "b": "Non-parametric tests (chi-square, Mann-Whitney U) are distribution-free alternatives for ranks or skewed data. Switching to them also leaves the error in the data untouched.", "d": "Estimating a standard error (\"standard error of deviation\" garbles standard error or standard deviation) measures how large the sampling error is likely to be; it quantifies error rather than reducing it."}}, {"q": "A statistical test that does not specify conditions of normality scores and do not need to have well-defined population is:", "concept": "The answer is b: a non-parametric test makes no assumption that scores are normally distributed and does not need well-defined population parameters. Tests fall into two families. A parametric test (the t-test, ANOVA, Pearson's r) estimates population parameters such as the mean and variance, and assumes that scores come from a normal population, that groups have similar variances and that data are on an interval or ratio scale. A non-parametric test, also called a distribution-free test, works on ranks, signs or category counts instead, so it suits nominal or ordinal data, small samples and clearly skewed data. Sidney Siegel (1956) popularised these tests in psychology with his book Nonparametric Statistics for the Behavioral Sciences. Common examples are chi-square (frequencies), the Mann-Whitney U test (two independent groups), the Wilcoxon signed-rank test (two related samples), the Kruskal-Wallis test (three or more groups) and Spearman's rank correlation. Example: Kabir has only eight children rank a set of cartoons from most to least liked. Ranks are ordinal and eight is too few to check normality, so he uses Spearman's rho rather than Pearson's r. The price of this freedom is lower power when the data really are normal. Option b is right because freedom from the normality assumption and from fixed population parameters is the defining feature of non-parametric tests. Exam trap: \"non-probability\" sounds like \"non-parametric\", but non-probability describes sampling methods, not statistical tests.", "traps": {"a": "A parametric test is the opposite: it assumes normally distributed scores, homogeneity of variance and interval data, and it estimates population parameters.", "c": "Random test is not a recognised family of statistical tests; the word random belongs to random sampling and random assignment, which concern how participants are chosen and allocated.", "d": "Non-probability refers to sampling methods (convenience, quota, purposive, snowball) in which not every member of the population has a known chance of selection. There is no class of non-probability tests."}}, {"q": "The MMPI-I consists of _____ validity scales and _____ clinical scales.", "concept": "The answer is d: the original MMPI has 3 validity scales and 10 clinical scales. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory was built by Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) through empirical criterion keying: an item was kept if psychiatric patient groups answered it differently from normal people, whatever the item seemed to be about. It has 566 true-false items. The 10 clinical scales are 1 Hypochondriasis (Hs), 2 Depression (D), 3 Hysteria (Hy), 4 Psychopathic deviate (Pd), 5 Masculinity-femininity (Mf), 6 Paranoia (Pa), 7 Psychasthenia (Pt), 8 Schizophrenia (Sc), 9 Hypomania (Ma) and 0 Social introversion (Si). The 3 validity scales check test-taking attitude. L (Lie) catches naive attempts to look good by denying common small faults. F (Infrequency) picks up rare, odd answers that suggest random responding, faking bad or severe disturbance. K (Correction), added in 1946 by Paul Meehl and Starke Hathaway, picks up subtler defensiveness and is used to correct some clinical scales. The Cannot Say (?) score, the number of items left blank, is also checked. The MMPI-2 (1989) has 567 items and added validity scales such as VRIN, TRIN and F(B), while keeping the 10 clinical scales. Option d is right because 3 validity and 10 clinical scales is the standard count for the original inventory. Exam trap: the later versions have many more validity scales, and the MMPI-2-RF has 9 Restructured Clinical scales, so larger or odd numbers can look familiar; the stem asks about the first MMPI. Key note: counting Cannot Say gives 4 validity indicators, but option c pairs that with 9 clinical scales, which is wrong; 3 and 10 is the standard count; d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Seven validity and 12 clinical scales matches no version of the MMPI. Even the MMPI-2, with its extra validity scales, keeps 10 clinical scales.", "b": "Five validity and 12 clinical scales is also invented; the inventory has never had 12 clinical scales.", "c": "Four validity scales is reachable only by counting Cannot Say alongside L, F and K, and 9 clinical scales is wrong for the original MMPI (9 is the number of Restructured Clinical scales in the much later MMPI-2-RF). The pair as a whole does not describe the MMPI."}}, {"q": "_____ validity is used for the purpose of determining whether the test score of people in one category are significantly different from those of people in other categories.", "concept": "The answer is d: concurrent validity is shown when a test separates people who already belong to different categories, such as patients and non-patients, at the time of testing. Validity asks whether a test measures what it claims to measure. In criterion-related validity, test scores are compared with an outside measure of the same quality, called the criterion. There are two kinds, and they differ only in timing. In concurrent validity the criterion is available now: the test is given alongside a present diagnosis, an established test or a current group membership. In predictive validity the criterion comes later, as when entrance scores are compared with marks at the end of the first year. Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955) defined concurrent validity as test and criterion measured at essentially the same time, named psychiatric diagnosis as a typical present criterion, and called the original MMPI's separation of patient groups from \"normals\" concurrent validity: if people in one category score significantly differently from people in another, the test reflects their present status. Example: Arjun builds a new depression questionnaire and gives it to 50 inpatients already diagnosed with depression and 50 college volunteers. The patients score much higher on the same day, so the questionnaire shows concurrent validity. Option d is right because the categories exist at the time of testing, which is the mark of concurrent validity. Exam trap: \"criterion\" (option a) is the parent category and so looks safe, but the stem asks for the specific type, and the comparison is made now, not against a future outcome. Key note: Cronbach and Meehl, and modern texts, also treat known-groups (contrasted groups) differences as evidence of construct validity, and \"criterion\" is the parent category of concurrent validity; the specific name for comparing present categories is concurrent validity; d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Criterion-related validity is the umbrella that covers both concurrent and predictive validity. It is not wrong in spirit, but the stem describes the specific, present-time type.", "b": "Predictive validity compares test scores with a criterion measured in the future, such as later job performance or grades. The categories in the stem already exist, so nothing is being predicted.", "c": "Content validity is judged by experts who check that the items cover the whole domain the test is meant to sample, as in an achievement test. It involves no comparison of groups' scores."}}, {"q": "Which among the following is not the method for estimating the reliability of tests?", "concept": "The answer is d: pair (paired) comparison is a scaling method, not a way of estimating the reliability of a test. Reliability is the consistency of test scores. In the classical test theory of Charles Spearman (1904), each observed score is a true score plus random error, and several methods estimate how much error a test carries. Test-retest gives the same test to the same people twice and correlates the two sets of scores, a coefficient of stability. Parallel (alternate) forms give two equivalent versions of the test, a coefficient of equivalence. Split-half divides one test into two halves (often odd and even items), correlates them, and steps the result up with the Spearman-Brown formula (Charles Spearman and William Brown, 1910), since each half is only half as long. Internal consistency formulas (G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson, 1937; Lee Cronbach's alpha, 1951) and inter-rater agreement complete the list. Paired comparison does something different: each object is paired with every other object, the judge picks one from each pair, and Louis Thurstone's (1927) law of comparative judgement turns the choices into scale values. With n objects there are n(n minus 1)/2 pairs. Example: Meera orders five job applicants by comparing each pair, making 10 comparisons; this ranks the applicants but says nothing about whether a test gives consistent scores. Option d is right because it is the only option that scales objects rather than checking score consistency. Exam trap: \"pair comparison\" sounds like correlating paired sets of scores, which is what reliability methods do, so it slips past a hurried reader.", "traps": {"a": "Test-retest is a reliability method: the same test is given twice to the same group, and the correlation between the two sets of scores is a coefficient of stability.", "b": "Parallel (alternate) forms is a reliability method: two equivalent versions of the test are given to the same group, giving a coefficient of equivalence.", "c": "Split-half is a reliability method: one test is split into two halves, and their correlation is stepped up with the Spearman-Brown formula to estimate the reliability of the full-length test."}}, {"q": "The first edition of the Binet-Simon intelligence scale was published in:", "concept": "The answer is b: the first Binet-Simon intelligence scale was published in 1905. In 1904 the French Ministry of Public Instruction set up a commission to find a way of identifying Paris schoolchildren who could not cope with ordinary lessons and needed special education. Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905), a psychologist and a physician, answered with 30 short tasks arranged in order of difficulty, from following a lit match with the eyes to defining abstract words. Unlike the sensory and reaction-time tests of Francis Galton (1884), they sampled higher mental processes such as judgement, comprehension and reasoning, which Binet saw as the core of intelligence. The 1908 revision grouped the items by the age at which most children passed them, creating the first age scale and the idea of mental age. The 1911 revision, the year Binet died, extended the scale to adults. William Stern (1912) proposed dividing mental age by chronological age, and Lewis Terman's Stanford-Binet (1916) multiplied this by 100 to give the intelligence quotient. Option b is right because 1905 is the date of the first edition. Exam trap: 1908 is the most tempting wrong date, because that revision introduced mental age, which many students remember as the scale's starting point; 1903 is the year of Binet's earlier book on intelligence, based on his two daughters.", "traps": {"a": "1903 is the year Alfred Binet published L'étude expérimentale de l'intelligence (The experimental study of intelligence), based on observations of his two daughters. It was a study, not the scale.", "c": "1908 is the year of the first revision, which arranged items by age level and introduced mental age. It was the second version, not the first edition.", "d": "1906 is a filler date; no Binet-Simon edition appeared that year. The versions came in 1905, 1908 and 1911."}}, {"q": "In OCD compulsion are generally thought to be:", "concept": "The answer is c: compulsions are repetitive or ritualised behaviours, or mental acts, that a person feels driven to perform to prevent a feared outcome or to reduce anxiety. Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) has two parts. Obsessions are intrusive, unwanted thoughts, urges or images (of contamination, harm, doubt, or forbidden sexual or religious themes) that cause marked anxiety. Compulsions are the responses: visible acts such as washing, checking, ordering and tapping, or hidden mental acts such as counting, praying or repeating words silently. DSM-5 says they are done in response to an obsession or according to rigid rules, aimed at preventing or reducing distress or some dreaded event, yet they are either not realistically connected to that event or clearly excessive. The relief they bring is brief, so they are negatively reinforced and grow stronger. Diagnosis needs the symptoms to be time-consuming (more than an hour a day) or to cause marked distress or impairment. Example: Kabir has the obsession that his flat will catch fire if the gas is left on. He checks the knob 20 times before leaving, counting aloud, and feels he must start again if interrupted. The checking is the compulsion; the fear of fire is the obsession. Option c is right because it captures the repetitive, ritualised act and its purpose of preventing a negative outcome. Exam trap: option a describes harm obsessions, so students who mix up the two halves of OCD pick it; obsessions are thoughts that arrive, compulsions are what the person does to neutralise them.", "traps": {"a": "Repetitive thoughts of harming others are harm obsessions, the intrusive-thought half of OCD. Compulsions are the acts done in response, not the thoughts themselves.", "b": "An overwhelming desire to behave inappropriately describes an impulse, as in impulse-control disorders such as kleptomania or intermittent explosive disorder, where acting brings gratification or release. Compulsions are unwanted and are done to reduce anxiety, not out of desire.", "d": "Ritualised worrying about the negative outcome of events describes worry, the core of generalised anxiety disorder. Worry is repetitive thinking about realistic concerns, not a ritual performed to prevent the outcome."}}, {"q": "One of the two key steps of most basic form of the experimental method involves:", "concept": "The answer is a: one of the two key steps of the basic experimental method is that the presence or strength of some variable believed to affect behaviour is systematically altered. In the experimental method, as Robert Baron and Donn Byrne describe it in their social psychology textbook, the researcher does two things. First, the presence or strength of some factor thought to influence behaviour is deliberately changed; this factor is the independent variable (IV). Second, the effects of these changes, if any, are carefully measured; the measured behaviour is the dependent variable (DV). If the DV shifts when only the IV has been changed, the researcher can conclude that the IV caused the shift. Two further conditions make that conclusion safe: random assignment of participants to conditions, so that the groups start out equal, and holding every other factor constant. For example, Meera plays background music to one group of students and keeps another in silence (step 1, altering the IV), then counts the sums each student solves correctly (step 2, measuring the DV). Option a is right because it names the first step, the systematic alteration of a variable believed to affect behaviour; the printed option is cut short before the words 'is systematically altered', but it is plainly step 1. Exam trap: random assignment (option c) is essential to a valid experiment, so examiners offer it as a step, but it is a condition for interpreting the results, not one of the two steps; and measuring variables to see whether they are related (option d) is the correlational method, which alters nothing.", "traps": {"b": "This reverses step 2: in an experiment the effects of altering the independent variable must be carefully measured, and a study that left them unmeasured could show nothing.", "c": "This describes random assignment of participants to conditions, an essential safeguard that makes the groups equivalent at the start; it is a condition of a valid experiment, not one of the two basic steps (altering the IV and measuring its effects).", "d": "This is the correlational method: variables are measured as they naturally occur to see whether they are related, with nothing deliberately altered, so it cannot show cause and effect."}}, {"q": "Procedure in which the researchers who have contacts with participants do not know the hypothesis under investigation is known as:", "concept": "The answer is b: a procedure in which the researchers who have contact with participants do not know the hypothesis under investigation is called a double-blind procedure. Researchers' expectations can leak into a study without anyone intending it. Robert Rosenthal (1966) showed that experimenters who expect a particular result can subtly bring it about, through tone of voice, smiles or the way they record answers; this is the experimenter expectancy effect. Participants, too, pick up cues about what a study is meant to show and behave accordingly, which Martin Orne (1962) called demand characteristics. In a single-blind study only the participants are kept unaware of their condition or of the hypothesis. In a double-blind study the people who run the sessions and collect the data are kept unaware as well, often by having a colleague who does not know the hypothesis or the group assignments test the participants. Drug trials are the classic case: neither the patient nor the doctor knows who received the active pill and who the placebo. For example, Arjun tests whether a herbal tea improves memory; his research assistant, who does not know which cups contain the tea or what Arjun predicts, gives the memory test, so her expectations cannot shape the scores. Option b is right because keeping the researchers in contact with participants ignorant of the hypothesis is exactly what double-blind means, and it guards against both expectancy effects and demand characteristics. Exam trap: option a, the experimenter effect, is the bias that the double-blind procedure is designed to prevent, not the procedure itself; examiners swap the problem for its remedy.", "traps": {"a": "The experimenter effect (Robert Rosenthal's experimenter expectancy effect) is the unintended influence of a researcher's expectations on participants' behaviour or on the recorded results; it is the problem that the double-blind procedure solves, not the procedure.", "c": "Systematic observation is a research method in which behaviour is watched and recorded with a pre-set coding scheme, in the field or the laboratory; it says nothing about keeping researchers unaware of the hypothesis.", "d": "Correlational research measures two or more variables as they occur to see whether they are related; it is a type of study, not a safeguard against researcher bias."}}, {"q": "Which of the following perspective focuses on the primacy of social processes and relativity?", "concept": "The answer is d: the constructionist (social constructionist) perspective focuses on the primacy of social processes and on relativity. Social constructionism holds that what we take to be knowledge, including ideas such as mental illness or adolescence, is not simply discovered in nature but built up through social interaction, language and shared practices. Two consequences follow. First, social processes come first (primacy): conversations, institutions and relations of power create the meanings we live by. Second, knowledge is relative: because it is made by people in a particular culture and historical period, there is no single fixed truth, and the same behaviour can be understood quite differently in another place or time. Peter Berger and Thomas Luckmann set out the idea in 'The Social Construction of Reality' (1966), and Kenneth Gergen (1985) brought it into psychology, arguing that psychological knowledge is itself historically and culturally situated. For example, whether Kabir's restless, outspoken behaviour is seen as spirited, naughty or a disorder depends on the shared meanings of his family, school and era, not on a fact waiting to be found. Option d is right because only the constructionist perspective makes social processes primary and treats knowledge as relative. Exam trap: the social and cultural perspective (option a) also stresses society and culture, so it looks close, but it treats behaviour as shaped by real cultural influences rather than treating knowledge itself as socially built and relative. Key note: the sociocultural perspective does emphasise social processes, so a has some pull; but the pairing of primacy of social processes with relativity is the hallmark of the constructionist perspective, and d stands.", "traps": {"a": "The social and cultural (sociocultural) perspective explains behaviour by the influence of culture, norms and social context; it treats these influences as real causes rather than holding that knowledge itself is socially constructed and relative.", "b": "The evolutionary perspective explains behaviour as adaptations shaped by natural selection over many generations; it looks for universal, inherited tendencies, the opposite of relativity.", "c": "The biological perspective explains behaviour through the brain, nervous system, genes and hormones; it makes biology, not social processes, primary."}}, {"q": "Confounding variables is:", "concept": "The answer is b: confounding variables are variables that the researcher fails to eliminate or control, so that they damage the internal validity of an experiment. In an experiment the researcher changes the independent variable (IV) and measures the dependent variable (DV), hoping that any change in the DV is caused by the IV alone. An extraneous variable is any other factor that could affect the DV. When such a factor happens to vary along with the IV, it becomes a confounding variable: the two are tangled together, and the researcher can no longer tell which of them produced the result. Internal validity is the confidence that the IV, and nothing else, caused the change; Donald Campbell and Julian Stanley (1963) listed the classic threats to it, such as history, maturation, testing and selection. Confounds are removed by random assignment, holding conditions constant, counterbalancing and matching. For example, Riya tests a new study method by teaching it to a morning class and comparing that class with an afternoon class taught the old way. If the morning class does better, she cannot tell whether the method or the time of day (fresher students) caused it: time of day is confounded with the method. Option b is right because it gives both the defining feature (an uncontrolled variable) and its consequence (loss of internal validity). Exam trap: option a describes the experimenter effect, one specific source of bias that can act as a confound but is not the definition of one; and option c describes correlational research, so 'both (B) and (C)' fails.", "traps": {"a": "This describes the experimenter (expectancy) effect identified by Robert Rosenthal (1966): the researcher's unintended influence on participants' behaviour. It is one possible source of bias, not the general definition of a confounding variable.", "c": "This describes the correlational method, in which two or more variables are measured to see how they are related; it is a research design, not a kind of variable.", "d": "Option d needs both b and c to be correct definitions; c describes correlational research, not confounding, so the combination fails."}}, {"q": "Law of closure is:", "concept": "The answer is a: the law of closure is the tendency to perceive objects as whole entities even though some parts may be missing. Closure is one of the Gestalt laws of perceptual organisation. Max Wertheimer (1923) set out these grouping laws, and Wolfgang Kohler (1929) and Kurt Koffka (1935) developed them; the Gestalt school's central claim is that the whole is different from the sum of its parts, because the visual system actively organises what it receives. By closure, the mind fills gaps to complete a familiar shape: a circle drawn with small breaks is still seen as a circle, a logo made of separate strokes is read as one picture, and a partly hidden word on a signboard is still read correctly. For example, Meera sees a dotted outline of a triangle in her notebook and at once sees a triangle, not three separate broken lines. The other main laws are proximity (things close together are grouped), similarity (things that look alike are grouped), continuity (elements on a smooth line or curve belong together), common fate (things moving together are grouped) and the master law of Prägnanz (we see the simplest, most stable organisation the stimulus allows). Option a is right because filling in missing parts to see a complete whole is the definition of closure. Exam trap: option b, seeing complex patterns in terms of simpler shapes, is the law of Prägnanz (simplicity or good figure), the overarching Gestalt principle that is often confused with closure; closure is specifically about completing gaps.", "traps": {"b": "This is the law of Prägnanz (simplicity or good figure): we perceive a pattern in the simplest, most regular way possible, as when two overlapping rings are seen as two circles. It is about simplification, not about completing missing parts.", "c": "This is the law of proximity: items located close together in space or time are seen as a group, as when evenly spaced pairs of dots are seen as pairs.", "d": "This is the law of continuity (good continuation): elements arranged along a smooth line or curve are seen as belonging together, so two crossing lines are seen as two continuous lines."}}, {"q": "A type of conditioning in which the presentation of the UCS precedes the presentations of the CS is known as:", "concept": "The answer is d: none of these, because an arrangement in which the unconditioned stimulus (UCS) comes before the conditioned stimulus (CS) is backward conditioning, and that name is not among the options. In Ivan Pavlov's (1927) classical conditioning, the timing of the CS (a bell) and the UCS (food) decides how well learning occurs. In delay conditioning the CS begins first and is still on when the UCS arrives; this is the most effective arrangement, because the CS works as a warning signal. In trace conditioning the CS starts and ends before the UCS, leaving a gap, so the animal must hold a memory trace of the CS; it is weaker. In simultaneous conditioning the CS and UCS begin together, and learning is poor. In backward conditioning the UCS comes first and the CS follows. Because the CS then predicts nothing (the important event has already happened), backward conditioning produces little or no conditioned response, and the CS may even come to signal that the UCS will not occur (inhibitory conditioning). For example, if Kabir's dog is given food and only then hears a bell, the bell does not come to make the dog salivate. Option d is right because none of the three named procedures puts the UCS first. Exam trap: 'simulation conditioning' is probably a misprint for simultaneous conditioning, but even that is not UCS first, since the two stimuli start together; and trace conditioning is a forward arrangement in which the CS comes first.", "traps": {"a": "Reconditioning is relearning a conditioned response after extinction by pairing the CS and UCS again; it is usually faster than the original learning (savings) and says nothing about the UCS coming before the CS.", "b": "No procedure called simulation conditioning exists; if it is a misprint for simultaneous conditioning, the CS and UCS begin at the same moment, which is still not the UCS coming first.", "c": "Trace conditioning is a forward arrangement: the CS starts and ends before the UCS, with a gap between them, so the CS still comes first."}}, {"q": "The production process is an important factor in:", "concept": "The answer is b: the production process is an important factor in observational learning. Albert Bandura's social learning theory (1977) explains how people learn by watching others (models). In the Bobo doll study of Albert Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross (1961), children who watched an adult punch and kick an inflatable doll later copied the same acts. Bandura named four processes that observational learning needs. Attention: the observer must notice the model and the relevant features of the act. Retention: the act is stored in memory as images and verbal descriptions. Production (motor reproduction): the stored memory is converted into actual behaviour, which needs the physical ability, and the act is refined through practice and feedback. Motivation: the observer needs a reason to perform, such as direct, vicarious or self-reinforcement. Motivation decides whether a learned act is actually performed, which is why Bandura separated learning from performance. For example, Arjun watches a video of a cricketer playing a cover drive, attends to the footwork and remembers the steps, but cannot play the shot until his body can carry out the movement; that third step is the production process. Option b is right because production is one of Bandura's four processes of observational learning. Exam trap: chaining and shaping (options c and d) both build up a behaviour step by step and so sound like producing a response, but they are operant techniques from B. F. Skinner's (1953) work, driven by reinforcement, not stages of learning by watching a model.", "traps": {"a": "A fixed-interval schedule is one of Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner's (1957) schedules of reinforcement: the first response after a set time is reinforced, producing a scalloped pattern of responding; no model or production stage is involved.", "c": "Chaining is an operant technique in which a sequence of responses is linked, each step reinforced by the chance to perform the next and the last by a reinforcer; it is learning by consequences, not by observation.", "d": "Shaping is an operant technique of reinforcing successive approximations to a target behaviour; it builds behaviour through reinforcement, not through attention, retention, production and motivation."}}, {"q": "The aspects of our physical states that serve as retrieval cues for information stored in long term memory is referred to as:", "concept": "The answer is d: the aspects of our physical (internal) states that serve as retrieval cues for information in long-term memory are referred to as state-dependent retrieval. Memories are stored together with the cues present at encoding, and recall improves when those cues are present again. By the encoding specificity principle of Endel Tulving and Donald Thomson (1973), a retrieval cue works to the extent that it was encoded along with the memory. Two kinds of cue are distinguished. In context-dependent memory the external setting is the cue: Duncan Godden and Alan Baddeley (1975) had divers learn words underwater or on the beach, and recall was best in the same place. In state-dependent memory the internal physical or mental state is the cue: Donald Goodwin and colleagues (1969) found that material learned while intoxicated was recalled better when intoxicated again, and Gordon Bower (1981) extended the idea to mood-dependent memory. For example, Riya revises late at night after two cups of strong coffee; she may recall more in the test if she is in the same caffeinated, aroused state, because her bodily state at retrieval matches her state at learning. Option d is right because the stem names our physical states as the cues, which defines state-dependent retrieval. Exam trap: context means outside (the room, the place) and state means inside (drugs, arousal, mood); option a is the external version, and option b is the broad principle behind both rather than the name for internal-state cues.", "traps": {"a": "Context-dependent memory is better recall when the external environment at retrieval matches that at learning, as in Duncan Godden and Alan Baddeley's divers; the stem asks about internal physical states, not surroundings.", "b": "The encoding specificity principle (Endel Tulving and Donald Thomson, 1973) is the general rule that a cue helps if it was encoded with the memory; it covers both context and state effects and is not the specific name for physical-state cues.", "c": "Procedural memory is long-term memory for skills and habits, such as riding a bicycle, shown in performance rather than conscious recall; it is a memory system, not a kind of retrieval cue."}}, {"q": "Yerkes Dodson's law states:", "concept": "The answer is c: the Yerkes-Dodson law states that the level of arousal beyond which performance begins to decline is a function of task difficulty. Robert Yerkes and John Dodson (1908) trained mice to choose between a light and a dark box, using electric shocks of different strengths for wrong choices. On the medium and hard discriminations learning was fastest with moderate shocks (on the easy one, the stronger the shock, the faster the learning), and the harder the discrimination, the weaker the shock that gave the best learning. The law as later taught says performance is an inverted-U function of arousal: too little arousal leaves a person drowsy and careless, a moderate amount gives the best performance, and too much makes the person tense and disorganised. The peak (the optimum) is not the same for every task: it is relatively high for simple or well-learned tasks and lower for difficult or new ones. For example, Kabir's exam-day nerves help him run fast in a school race (a simple task) but make his mind go blank on a difficult maths paper (a complex task). Option c is right because it states the distinctive claim of the law: the point at which more arousal starts to hurt depends on how hard the task is. Exam trap: option a, seeking an optimal rather than a minimal level of arousal, is optimal arousal theory (Donald Hebb, 1955); it shares the idea that moderate arousal is best, but it is a theory of motivation, not the law linking arousal, performance and task difficulty.", "traps": {"a": "This is optimal arousal theory (Donald Hebb, 1955): people are motivated to keep arousal at a moderate, optimal level rather than to reduce it to a minimum. It explains why we seek stimulation, not how the performance optimum shifts with task difficulty.", "b": "This is incentive theory: behaviour is pulled by external goals and the expectation of rewarding outcomes, such as money or praise, rather than pushed by inner drives.", "d": "This is drive-reduction theory (Clark Hull, 1943): biological needs create internal drives that push behaviour until the need is met; it says nothing about an optimal level of arousal or task difficulty."}}, {"q": "Sternberg's Triarchic theory of intelligence proposes _____ as the basic type of intelligence.", "concept": "The answer is d: Robert Sternberg's triarchic theory proposes componential, practical and creative intelligence as the basic types. In 'Beyond IQ' (1985) Robert Sternberg argued that IQ tests capture only part of intelligence and divided it into three parts (triarchic means ruled by three). Componential (analytical) intelligence covers the mental processes used in thinking: planning, monitoring, encoding and comparing information, the skills school tests reward. Experiential (creative) intelligence is the ability to deal with new situations and to make familiar tasks automatic, combining experiences in original ways. Contextual (practical) intelligence is street smarts: adapting to, shaping and selecting environments so as to succeed in everyday life. He later called the balanced use of analytical, creative and practical abilities successful intelligence. For example, Meera tops her written tests (componential), invents a new way to explain fractions to her younger brother (creative) and talks a shopkeeper into a fair price (practical). Option d is right because componential, practical and creative are exactly Sternberg's three intelligences, with creative standing for the experiential subtheory. Exam trap: option a slips one of Sternberg's terms (componential) in beside Raymond Cattell's fluid and crystallised intelligence (proposed 1943, tested 1963), so it looks half right; mixed lists like this are the usual bait.", "traps": {"a": "Fluid and crystallised intelligence come from Raymond Cattell's theory, later developed with John Horn; only componential belongs to Sternberg, so the list mixes two theories.", "b": "Technological and integral are not types of intelligence in any major theory; only practical is one of Sternberg's three.", "c": "Bodily-kinesthetic, musical and personal (intrapersonal and interpersonal) intelligences belong to Howard Gardner's (1983) theory of multiple intelligences, not to Sternberg's triarchic theory."}}, {"q": "_____ is a hormone which is involved in the breakdown of fats. It is released when food reaches the part of the intestine immediately below the stomach.", "concept": "The answer is a: cholecystokinin (CCK) is the hormone involved in the breakdown of fats that is released when food reaches the duodenum, the part of the small intestine immediately below the stomach. When partly digested food, especially fat and protein, passes from the stomach into the duodenum, cells in the intestinal wall release CCK into the blood. CCK has two main digestive effects: it makes the gallbladder contract and release bile, which breaks fat into small droplets (emulsification), and it makes the pancreas release digestive enzymes such as lipase, which split fats into fatty acids. It also slows the emptying of the stomach. For psychologists CCK matters as a satiety signal: James Gibbs, R. C. Young and Gerard Smith (1973) injected CCK into hungry rats and found that they ate smaller meals, so it helps to end a meal. It works alongside other signals: ghrelin from the empty stomach starts hunger, and leptin from fat cells signals long-term energy stores. For example, after Arjun eats a rich paneer dish, CCK released from his duodenum helps digest the fat and adds to the full feeling that makes him stop eating. Option a is right because CCK is the duodenal hormone linked to fat digestion. Exam trap: catecholamine (option d) tempts because epinephrine and norepinephrine really do help break down stored body fat (lipolysis) in stress or fasting; but they come from the adrenal medulla and sympathetic nerves, and they are a class of chemicals, not the hormone released when food reaches the duodenum.", "traps": {"b": "Estradiol is the main oestrogen, made chiefly by the ovaries; it governs female sexual development and the menstrual cycle, not fat digestion.", "c": "Antidiuretic hormone (vasopressin) is released by the posterior pituitary and makes the kidneys retain water; it regulates fluid balance and thirst, not the breakdown of fats.", "d": "Catecholamines (dopamine, norepinephrine and epinephrine) are a class of monoamine neurotransmitters and hormones; epinephrine and norepinephrine from the adrenal medulla prepare the body for fight or flight and do promote the breakdown of stored body fat, but none is released by the duodenum when food arrives."}}, {"q": "According to Cannon-Bard theory:", "concept": "The answer is c: according to the Cannon-Bard theory, felt emotions and bodily changes occur in parallel with each other, both resulting from activity in brain areas. William James (1884) and Carl Lange (1885) had argued that the body reacts first and the felt emotion is our perception of those bodily changes: we feel afraid because we tremble. Walter Cannon (1927) attacked this view: emotion survives when the viscera are cut off from the brain, visceral changes are too slow and too similar across emotions to tell fear from anger, and injected adrenaline produces arousal without real emotion. Cannon's own alternative, which Philip Bard (1928) extended through experiments on rage in cats, was that an emotional stimulus is processed in the brain (Cannon pointed to the thalamus), which sends signals at the same time to the cortex, producing the conscious feeling, and to the body, producing arousal. Feeling and bodily change therefore happen together and independently; neither causes the other. Later research placed the key structures in the hypothalamus and limbic system rather than the thalamus, but the parallel idea remains. For example, when Riya sees a snake on the path, her fear and her racing heart arise at the same moment from the same brain signal. Option c is right because parallel feeling and arousal, both produced by brain activity, is the core claim of the theory. Exam trap: option a is something Cannon really said, so it feels familiar, but it was his criticism of the James-Lange theory, not the Cannon-Bard theory's own account. Key note: a is a true statement made by Cannon, but as his objection to James-Lange; the Cannon-Bard theory's own claim is c, and c stands.", "traps": {"a": "This restates one of Walter Cannon's (1927) objections to the James-Lange theory (much the same visceral changes occur in very different emotions); it is a criticism of a rival theory, not the Cannon-Bard account of how emotion arises.", "b": "This is the James-Lange theory (William James, 1884; Carl Lange, 1885): emotion is the perception of bodily changes, so the body comes first, the very sequence Cannon and Bard rejected.", "d": "This is appraisal theory (Magda Arnold, 1960; Richard Lazarus, 1966): emotion follows a cognitive evaluation of what the situation means for the person, a cognitive account rather than one of parallel brain activation."}}, {"q": "Libido is wholly intrapsychic. It attaches itself to the mental representation of objects satisfying instinctual needs. This process is called:", "concept": "The answer is c: the process by which libido attaches itself to the mental representation of objects that satisfy instinctual needs is called cathexis. From his earliest writings in the 1890s, Sigmund Freud saw the mind as an energy system. The instincts supply psychic energy, and the energy of the life instincts is called libido. Libido is wholly intrapsychic: it does not flow into the real person or thing but into the inner image (mental representation) of it. Investing energy in such a representation is cathexis, the word James Strachey used to translate Freud's German Besetzung (occupation or charge). A hungry infant cathects the image of the breast; an adult may cathect a lover, a cause or a hobby. The ego and superego can oppose an id cathexis with an anticathexis, a counter-investment of energy that keeps a wish repressed, which is why repression is costly: energy spent holding a wish back is lost to work and love. For example, Kabir's libido is cathected to his idea of a famous cricketer, so thoughts of the cricketer feel charged and absorbing, and much of his attention flows towards them. Option c is right because cathexis names exactly the attaching of libido to an object's mental representation. Exam trap: catharsis (option d) looks and sounds like cathexis, but it is the discharge of pent-up emotion, as in the cathartic method of Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud (1895), not the binding of energy to an object.", "traps": {"a": "Psychic energy is the energy itself (libido is the energy of the life instincts), not the process of attaching it to an object's mental representation.", "b": "Drive reduction is Clark Hull's (1943) theory of motivation and learning: behaviour that reduces a biological drive is reinforced; it is a behaviourist concept, not a Freudian process of investing libido.", "d": "Catharsis is the release or discharge of pent-up emotion, used by Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud (1895) in the cathartic method and by Aristotle for the effect of tragedy; it is a discharge of feeling, not the attaching of libido to an object."}}, {"q": "Introjection is:", "concept": "The answer is a: introjection is unconsciously incorporating someone else's values into one's own personality. It is both a defence mechanism and a process of development in psychoanalytic theory. The term was introduced by Sandor Ferenczi (1909) and adopted by Sigmund Freud (1915), and Anna Freud (1936) listed it among the defences in 'The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence'. In introjection a person swallows whole the attitudes, standards or traits of another person, usually a powerful or loved one, so that they feel like one's own rather than borrowed. Children introject their parents' rules and prohibitions, and in Freud's account this is how the superego forms. It also eases anxiety: by taking in a feared person's standards, one feels less threatened by that person (Anna Freud's identification with the aggressor builds on it). Introjection is the opposite of projection, which pushes one's own unacceptable feelings out onto others. For example, Meera grew up hearing her father say that wasting food is shameful; as an adult she feels real guilt when she leaves food on her plate, as if the rule were her own idea. Option a is right because it defines introjection as the unconscious taking in of another person's values. Exam trap: introjection and projection sound like a pair and are often reversed; introjection brings others' qualities in, while projection pushes one's own impulses out.", "traps": {"b": "This is repression, Freud's primary defence: keeping threatening thoughts, memories or impulses out of conscious awareness; nothing is taken in from another person.", "c": "This is rationalisation: giving plausible but false reasons to justify unacceptable behaviour, such as blaming an unfair paper for a failure that came from not studying.", "d": "This is displacement: shifting an impulse or feeling from its real target to a safer, less threatening one, as when anger at a boss is taken out on a younger sibling."}}, {"q": "NEO-PI-R was developed by:", "concept": "The answer is c: the NEO-PI-R (Revised NEO Personality Inventory) was developed by Paul Costa and Robert McCrae (1992). Paul Costa and Robert McCrae worked at the US National Institute on Aging, studying how personality changes with age. Their first inventory measured three broad domains, Neuroticism, Extraversion and Openness to experience, which gave the name NEO. The NEO Personality Inventory of 1985 added Agreeableness and Conscientiousness, so that it covered all five factors of the Five-Factor Model (the Big Five). The 1992 revision, the NEO-PI-R, has 240 items answered on a five-point agree-disagree scale and scores five domains, each with six facets, giving 30 facets in all; Neuroticism, for instance, includes anxiety, angry hostility, depression, self-consciousness, impulsiveness and vulnerability. Scores are reported as T scores. A short 60-item NEO Five-Factor Inventory (NEO-FFI) had appeared in 1989, and the NEO-PI-3 (2005) followed. For example, Kabir's NEO-PI-R profile at a counselling centre shows high Conscientiousness, especially on self-discipline and achievement striving, and low Neuroticism. Option c is right because the NEO-PI-R and its 1992 date belong to Costa and McCrae. Exam trap: the other options pair real surnames with the wrong partner or date; Robert McCrae did write with Oliver John on the five-factor model, and Hans Eysenck built a rival model of personality, but neither partnership made the NEO-PI-R.", "traps": {"a": "Costa and Costa (1984) is an invented pairing: Paul Costa's co-author was Robert McCrae, and 1984 matches no edition (the NEO-PI appeared in 1985 and the NEO-PI-R in 1992).", "b": "Robert McCrae never co-authored the NEO-PI-R with Hans Eysenck; Eysenck built his own three-factor PEN model and the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire (1975, with Sybil Eysenck).", "d": "Robert McCrae and Oliver John wrote a well-known introduction to the five-factor model (1992), and Oliver John developed the Big Five Inventory, but they did not develop the NEO-PI-R, and 1988 is not its date."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a projective test?", "concept": "The answer is a: the word association test is the option set apart here as not a projective test, although strictly all four options are projective. Projective tests use ambiguous or open stimuli onto which people project their needs and conflicts. Francis Galton (1879) timed the associations that a list of words called up in his own mind, and Carl Jung (1910) made word association a clinical tool: 100 stimulus words, each reply timed, with complex indicators (a long delay, no reply, an odd answer) betraying an emotionally charged complex. Gardner Lindzey (1959) sorted projective techniques by the response they demand: association (word association and Hermann Rorschach's (1921) inkblots), construction (the Thematic Apperception Test of Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray, 1935), completion (sentence completion, such as Julian Rotter's Incomplete Sentences Blank, 1950), choice or ordering, and expression. Of the four options, word association is the most controlled: a fixed list, a one-word reply, and scoring that leans on reaction time. For example, when the tester says 'mother' and Riya pauses six seconds before saying 'kind', the delay is read as a complex indicator. Option a is right because word association is the most controlled, least open-ended of the four, so it is the one set apart. Exam trap: many pick sentence completion, whose stems steer the answer; but in theory questions, treat word association as Lindzey does, as a projective association technique. Key note: strictly all four options are projective (in Lindzey's scheme word association and the Rorschach are association techniques, the TAT a construction technique and sentence completion a completion technique), so the item is flawed. Word association is keyed because it is the answer this same item was given on the RML 2021 paper and it is the most controlled of the four (a single-word response to a fixed list, from Francis Galton's 1879 experiment and Carl Jung's diagnostic association studies), and some texts instead call sentence completion semi-projective; the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"b": "Hermann Rorschach's (1921) inkblot test shows ten ambiguous, symmetrical inkblots and asks what each might be; it is a classic projective technique (an association technique in Lindzey's scheme), so it cannot be the one that is not projective.", "c": "The Thematic Apperception Test (Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray, 1935) asks for stories about ambiguous pictures, which reveal the teller's needs, press and conflicts; it is a classic projective technique (a construction technique in Lindzey's scheme).", "d": "The sentence completion test, such as Julian Rotter's Incomplete Sentences Blank (1950), asks the person to finish stems like 'I worry about...'; it is a classic projective technique (a completion technique in Lindzey's scheme), even though its stems make it more structured and some texts call it semi-projective."}}, {"q": "\"Anyone who either cannot lead the common life and therefore does not partake of the society is either a beast or God\": these famous lines are by:", "concept": "The answer is a: the lines about a beast or a god are by Aristotle. In 'Politics' (Book I, written about 350 BCE), Aristotle (384 to 322 BCE) argued that man is by nature a social animal (literally a political animal, a creature of the polis, the city-state). He added that anyone who cannot share in the common life, or is so self-sufficient as not to need it, is no part of the state and must be either a beast or a god. His point is that humans become fully human only in a community; to live outside it is to be less than human (a beast) or more than human (a god). Social psychology textbooks quote him to show that interest in human social nature is ancient, long before social psychology became a science in the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries. Aristotle also wrote on persuasion in the 'Rhetoric' (ethos, pathos and logos), which anticipates modern work on attitude change. For example, when Arjun moves to a new city and feels lost until he makes friends, his experience fits Aristotle's claim that people need a community to flourish. Option a is right because the beast-or-god passage comes from Aristotle's 'Politics'. Exam trap: Plato was Aristotle's teacher and Socrates was Plato's, so their names are offered as near-misses; Plato also wrote about the ideal society in the 'Republic', but the beast-or-god sentence is Aristotle's.", "traps": {"b": "Socrates (about 470 to 399 BCE), known through Plato's dialogues, is linked with 'know thyself' and 'the unexamined life is not worth living'; he left no writings, and the beast-or-god sentence is not his.", "c": "Plato (about 428 to 348 BCE) described the ideal state in the 'Republic' and taught Aristotle, but the beast-or-god passage comes from his pupil's 'Politics'.", "d": "Auguste Comte (1798 to 1857) coined the word sociology and founded positivism in the nineteenth century, more than two thousand years after these lines were written."}}, {"q": "_____ is often credited with the first experiment in social psychology.", "concept": "The answer is d: Norman Triplett (1898) is often credited with the first experiment in social psychology. Norman Triplett, working at Indiana University, studied cycling records and noticed that cyclists rode faster when paced by another rider or racing a rival than when riding alone against the clock. To test whether the presence of others speeds people up, he had children turn fishing reels as fast as they could, sometimes alone and sometimes beside another child doing the same task. Many wound faster in company, though some did worse. Published in 1898 in the American Journal of Psychology, the study began research on what Floyd Allport (1920) named social facilitation, later explained by Robert Zajonc (1965): the presence of others raises arousal, which helps on easy, well-learned tasks and hurts on hard or new ones. For example, Meera runs her best time when racing beside a classmate rather than alone. Option d is right because Triplett's reel-winding study is the standard first experiment in social psychology textbooks. Exam trap: William McDougall and Edward Alsworth Ross (both 1908) are firsts too, but for the first two textbooks titled social psychology, not for the first experiment; and Max Ringelmann's rope-pulling studies show effort falling in groups (social loafing), the opposite effect. Key note: some historians (David Kravitz and Barbara Martin, 1986) point out that Ringelmann's rope-pulling work was done in the 1880s, before Triplett's, though published only in 1913; but the standard answer credits Triplett, and d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Max Ringelmann's rope-pulling studies (done in the 1880s, published in 1913) found that each person pulls less hard as the group grows, the effect later called social loafing (Bibb Latane, Kipling Williams and Stephen Harkins, 1979); textbooks do not usually credit them as the first social psychology experiment.", "b": "William McDougall (1908) wrote 'An Introduction to Social Psychology', one of the first two textbooks with that title, explaining social behaviour by inborn instincts; it was a book, not an experiment.", "c": "Edward Alsworth Ross (1908), a sociologist, wrote the other early textbook, 'Social Psychology', built on imitation and suggestion; again a book, not an experiment."}}, {"q": "Hermeneutic is the:", "concept": "The answer is a: hermeneutics is the science (theory and method) of interpreting communication, whether verbal, non-verbal or textual. The word comes from the Greek hermeneuein, to interpret, and is linked with Hermes, the messenger who carried and explained the messages of the gods. Hermeneutics began as the art of interpreting sacred and legal texts. Friedrich Schleiermacher (1768 to 1834) widened it into a general theory of understanding any text or speech, and Wilhelm Dilthey (1883) made it the method of the human sciences: the natural sciences explain from outside, but the human sciences must understand (Verstehen) meanings from inside. Martin Heidegger (1927) and Hans-Georg Gadamer (1960) argued that all understanding is interpretation shaped by the interpreter's own background. A central idea is the hermeneutic circle: we understand the parts of a text through the whole and the whole through its parts. In psychology, hermeneutics underlies qualitative methods such as Jonathan Smith's (1996) interpretative phenomenological analysis, with its double hermeneutic: the researcher interprets the participant's own interpretation of an experience. For example, when Riya analyses interviews with new mothers, she reads each remark in the light of the whole interview, and the interview in the light of each remark. Option a is right because interpreting the meaning of communication is exactly what hermeneutics studies. Exam trap: options b and c borrow phrases from memory research and from the definition of social psychology, so they sound like textbook definitions, but neither is about interpreting meaning.", "traps": {"b": "This echoes research on how memory shapes our understanding of the world, as in Frederic Bartlett's (1932) reconstructive memory and schemas; it belongs to cognitive psychology, not to the theory of interpretation.", "c": "This echoes Gordon Allport's (1954) definition of social psychology: how thoughts, feelings and behaviour are influenced by the actual, imagined or implied presence of others; it is not hermeneutics.", "d": "None of the above fails because option a correctly defines hermeneutics as the science of interpreting verbal, non-verbal and textual communication."}}, {"q": "An experiment where the goal was to observe if the authority position would cause people to act differently was carried out by:", "concept": "The answer is b: the experiment whose goal was to see whether a position of authority would make people act differently was carried out by Philip Zimbardo, the Stanford Prison Experiment (1971). In August 1971 Philip Zimbardo turned a basement corridor of the psychology building at Stanford University into a mock prison. He picked 24 male college students who had tested as the most stable and healthy of about 75 volunteers, and a coin toss made each one a guard or a prisoner, so any difference in how they behaved could not come from their personalities. Guards got khaki uniforms, whistles, wooden batons and mirrored sunglasses; prisoners were picked up at home by real police, given smocks, numbers instead of names and a chain on one ankle. Zimbardo himself played the prison superintendent. Within a day the prisoners rebelled, and the guards answered with sleep loss, push-ups, solitary confinement in a closet and humiliation, some of it worse at night when they thought no one was watching. Several prisoners broke down and had to be released. The study was planned for two weeks but was stopped after six days, after Christina Maslach, who had just finished her Stanford PhD, objected to what she saw. Craig Haney, Curtis Banks and Zimbardo (1973) concluded that the situation and the roles, not the people, produced the cruelty; Zimbardo later called this the Lucifer Effect (2007). Later critics, such as Stephen Reicher and Alexander Haslam's BBC Prison Study (2006) and Thibault Le Texier (2018), argue the guards were coached to be tough, but the textbook reading is still that a position of authority changes how ordinary people act. For example, Rohan, a quiet hostel student, is made warden of the junior block and within weeks is shouting at juniors and inventing new rules; the position, not his personality, changed how he acts. Option b is right because the Stanford Prison Experiment put ordinary people into a position of authority (or powerlessness) to see whether the position itself would change their behaviour. Exam trap: Stanley Milgram's obedience study (1963) is also about authority, but it asked whether people would obey orders from someone in authority, not whether being put in a position of authority changes how you act. Key note: until 25 Nov 2026 this mock keyed a, Milgram, reading the stem as obeying an authority. The stem's 'authority position' is the position the participants themselves were placed in, which is Zimbardo's question, and the usual summary of his study is that when people assume positions of authority they act the role, so the key is now b. If an examiner meant obedience to an authority figure the answer would be Milgram, so learn both.", "traps": {"a": "Stanley Milgram (1963) tested obedience: at Yale, 26 of 40 men (65 percent) went on giving what they thought were 450-volt shocks because an experimenter in a lab coat told them to. The participants obeyed someone else's authority; they were never placed in a position of authority themselves.", "c": "Solomon Asch (1951, 1956) studied conformity: participants judging line lengths often gave the same wrong answer as a unanimous group of peers; the pressure came from equals, not from a position of authority.", "d": "Muzafer Sherif (1935) studied how group norms form using the autokinetic effect (a still point of light that seems to move in a dark room), and later intergroup conflict in the Robbers Cave study (1954); neither put people in positions of authority."}}]});
